        ['Describe how you would obtain pure, dry crystals of copper(II) sulphate from a mixture of copper(II) sulphate and sand.', ['Dissolve in water', 'Filter off sand', 'Concentrate the filtrate', 'Cool to crystallise', 'Filter, wash and dry crystals'], 'Add water to the mixture and stir so that the copper(II) sulphate dissolves. Filter: the sand stays as the residue and the blue copper(II) sulphate solution is the filtrate. Heat the filtrate gently to evaporate some water until a drop placed on a cold tile forms crystals. Leave the hot saturated solution to cool so crystals form. Filter off the crystals, wash them with a little cold distilled water and dry them between filter papers.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['(a) Draw and label the apparatus for simple distillation. (b) Explain how it separates water from a salt solution. (c) State why fractional distillation is needed for ethanol and water.', ['Labelled diagram', 'Water vaporises and condenses', 'Salt left behind', 'Close boiling points', 'Fractionating column'], '(a) Labelled parts: distillation flask with the solution, heat source, thermometer at the side arm, Liebig condenser with water in at the bottom and out at the top, and a receiver for the distillate. (b) The solution is heated; water boils and its vapour passes into the condenser, where it cools and condenses into pure water. The salt does not evaporate and stays in the flask. (c) Ethanol and water have close boiling points, so both evaporate together; a fractionating column gives repeated condensation and evaporation so that the more volatile ethanol reaches the top first.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain how paper chromatography can show whether a food colouring is a single dye or a mixture, and how Rf values identify the dyes.', ['Pencil baseline and spot', 'Solvent below baseline', 'Dyes travel different distances', 'One spot = pure', 'Rf formula and comparison'], 'Put a spot of the colouring on a pencil baseline on chromatography paper. Stand the paper in a solvent with the solvent level below the baseline and cover it. As the solvent rises, each dye moves at a different rate depending on its solubility and attraction to the paper. A single dye gives one spot; a mixture gives several spots. Rf = distance moved by spot ÷ distance moved by solvent; comparing Rf values with known dyes under the same conditions identifies them.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['State five safety rules to follow in a chemistry laboratory and give a reason for each.', ['Goggles', 'No eating', 'Hair and flames', 'Acid to water', 'Fume cupboard'], '1. Wear safety goggles, to protect the eyes from splashes. 2. Never eat or drink in the laboratory, to avoid swallowing chemicals. 3. Tie back long hair and keep the Bunsen flame attended, to avoid burns. 4. Add concentrated acid to water and not water to acid, to avoid violent spitting. 5. Use a fume cupboard for toxic gases, to avoid breathing them in. Also: report breakages and spills to the teacher, and wash hands after practical work.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 2, 'theory', 5],
        ['A student wants to separate a mixture of sand, salt and iron filings. Describe, step by step, how each component can be obtained.', ['Magnet for iron', 'Dissolve salt', 'Filter out sand', 'Dry sand', 'Evaporate to get salt'], 'Use a magnet to remove the iron filings. Add water to the remaining mixture and stir to dissolve the salt. Filter: the sand is the residue; wash and dry it. Evaporate the filtrate (or crystallise it) to recover the salt.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
