        ['Write the s, p notation for potassium (Z = 19): ___', ['1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s¹', '1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶4s¹'], '', 'Fill 1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p, 4s in order.', 4, 'fill'],
        ['Naturally occurring chlorine consists of 80% of chlorine-35 and 20% of chlorine-37. (a) Define the term isotopy. (b) Calculate the relative atomic mass of chlorine. (c) Explain why both isotopes react in the same way.', ['Definition', 'Weighted average set-up', 'RAM 35.4', 'Same electron configuration'], '(a) Isotopy is the existence of atoms of the same element with the same atomic number but different mass numbers, because they have different numbers of neutrons. (b) RAM = (80 × 35 + 20 × 37) ÷ 100 = 35.4. (c) Both isotopes have the same number of electrons and the same electron configuration, and chemical reactions involve electrons.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Naturally occurring copper consists of 75% of copper-63 and 25% of copper-65. (a) Define the term isotopy. (b) Calculate the relative atomic mass of copper. (c) Explain why both isotopes react in the same way.', ['Definition', 'Weighted average set-up', 'RAM 63.5', 'Same electron configuration'], '(a) Isotopy is the existence of atoms of the same element with the same atomic number but different mass numbers, because they have different numbers of neutrons. (b) RAM = (75 × 63 + 25 × 65) ÷ 100 = 63.5. (c) Both isotopes have the same number of electrons and the same electron configuration, and chemical reactions involve electrons.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Naturally occurring chlorine consists of 60% of chlorine-35 and 40% of chlorine-37. (a) Define the term isotopy. (b) Calculate the relative atomic mass of chlorine. (c) Explain why both isotopes react in the same way.', ['Definition', 'Weighted average set-up', 'RAM 35.8', 'Same electron configuration'], '(a) Isotopy is the existence of atoms of the same element with the same atomic number but different mass numbers, because they have different numbers of neutrons. (b) RAM = (60 × 35 + 40 × 37) ÷ 100 = 35.8. (c) Both isotopes have the same number of electrons and the same electron configuration, and chemical reactions involve electrons.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Naturally occurring bromine consists of 60% of bromine-79 and 40% of bromine-81. (a) Define the term isotopy. (b) Calculate the relative atomic mass of bromine. (c) Explain why both isotopes react in the same way.', ['Definition', 'Weighted average set-up', 'RAM 79.8', 'Same electron configuration'], '(a) Isotopy is the existence of atoms of the same element with the same atomic number but different mass numbers, because they have different numbers of neutrons. (b) RAM = (60 × 79 + 40 × 81) ÷ 100 = 79.8. (c) Both isotopes have the same number of electrons and the same electron configuration, and chemical reactions involve electrons.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Naturally occurring lithium consists of 69% of lithium-6 and 31% of lithium-7. (a) Define the term isotopy. (b) Calculate the relative atomic mass of lithium. (c) Explain why both isotopes react in the same way.', ['Definition', 'Weighted average set-up', 'RAM 6.31', 'Same electron configuration'], '(a) Isotopy is the existence of atoms of the same element with the same atomic number but different mass numbers, because they have different numbers of neutrons. (b) RAM = (69 × 6 + 31 × 7) ÷ 100 = 6.31. (c) Both isotopes have the same number of electrons and the same electron configuration, and chemical reactions involve electrons.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
    ],
    'SHS 1: Mole calculations, empirical and molecular formulae' => [
        ['One mole of a substance contains', ['6.02 × 10²³ particles', '6.02 × 10²² particles', '22.4 particles', '1,000 particles'], 'A', 'Avogadro\'s constant.', 1],
        ['The empirical formula of a compound is', ['the actual number of atoms in one molecule', 'the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms in it', 'its structural formula', 'its molar mass'], 'B', 'For example CH₂O for glucose.', 2],
        ['The molecular formula of a compound with empirical formula CH₂ and molar mass 42 g/mol is', ['C₂H₄', 'CH₂', 'C₃H₆', 'C₄H₈'], 'C', '42 ÷ 14 = 3.', 3],
        ['The unit of molar mass is', ['mol/g', 'g', 'mol/dm³', 'g/mol'], 'D', 'Mass per mole.', 1],
        ['Percentage of nitrogen in NH₄NO₃ (N = 14, H = 1, O = 16) is', ['35%', '17.5%', '28%', '50%'], 'A', '28 ÷ 80 × 100.', 4],
        ['How many molecules are in 3 mol of aluminium oxide? (L = 6.02 × 10²³)', ['1.81 × 10²³', '1.81 × 10²⁴', '2.01 × 10²³', '1.81 × 10²⁵'], 'B', '3 × 6.02 × 10²³.', 2],
        ['How many moles are in 204 g of aluminium oxide, Al₂O₃?', ['20 mol', '1 mol', '2 mol', '4 mol'], 'C', 'Moles = mass ÷ Mr = 204 ÷ 102.', 2],
        ['How many molecules are in 2 mol of calcium carbonate? (L = 6.02 × 10²³)', ['1.2 × 10²³', '3.01 × 10²³', '1.2 × 10²⁵', '1.2 × 10²⁴'], 'D', '2 × 6.02 × 10²³.', 2],
        ['How many moles are in 11.2 g of potassium hydroxide, KOH?', ['0.2 mol', '2 mol', '0.1 mol', '0.4 mol'], 'A', 'Moles = mass ÷ Mr = 11.2 ÷ 56.', 2],
        ['What is the mass of 0.5 mol of sodium chloride, NaCl?', ['117 g', '29.25 g', '58.5 g', '30.25 g'], 'B', 'Mass = moles × Mr = 0.5 × 58.5.', 2],
        ['What volume does 2 mol of sulphur dioxide gas occupy at s.t.p.? (molar volume = 22.4 dm³)', ['11.2 dm³', '48 dm³', '44.8 dm³', '2 dm³'], 'C', '2 × 22.4.', 2],
        ['What volume does 0.2 mol of hydrogen chloride gas occupy at s.t.p.? (molar volume = 22.4 dm³)', ['112 dm³', '4.8 dm³', '0.2 dm³', '4.48 dm³'], 'D', '0.2 × 22.4.', 2],
        ['How many molecules are in 0.25 mol of glucose? (L = 6.02 × 10²³)', ['1.51 × 10²³', '1.51 × 10²²', '2.41 × 10²⁴', '1.51 × 10²⁴'], 'A', '0.25 × 6.02 × 10²³.', 2],
        ['What is the mass of 0.5 mol of potassium hydroxide, KOH?', ['112 g', '28 g', '56 g', '29 g'], 'B', 'Mass = moles × Mr = 0.5 × 56.', 2],
        ['How many moles are in 200 g of calcium carbonate, CaCO₃?', ['20 mol', '1 mol', '2 mol', '4 mol'], 'C', 'Moles = mass ÷ Mr = 200 ÷ 100.', 2],
        ['What is the mass of 0.1 mol of nitrogen, N₂?', ['280 g', '28 g', '5.6 g', '2.8 g'], 'D', 'Mass = moles × Mr = 0.1 × 28.', 2],
        ['How many molecules are in 2 mol of aluminium oxide? (L = 6.02 × 10²³)', ['1.2 × 10²⁴', '1.2 × 10²³', '3.01 × 10²³', '1.2 × 10²⁵'], 'A', '2 × 6.02 × 10²³.', 2],
        ['How many moles are in 19.6 g of sulphuric acid, H₂SO₄?', ['2 mol', '0.2 mol', '0.1 mol', '0.4 mol'], 'B', 'Moles = mass ÷ Mr = 19.6 ÷ 98.', 2],
        ['How many molecules are in 2 mol of sulphur dioxide? (L = 6.02 × 10²³)', ['1.2 × 10²³', '3.01 × 10²³', '1.2 × 10²⁴', '1.2 × 10²⁵'], 'C', '2 × 6.02 × 10²³.', 2],
        ['One mole of oxygen molecules has a mass of 16 g.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'O₂ has a molar mass of 32 g/mol.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['The empirical and molecular formulae of water are the same.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'H₂O cannot be simplified.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Equal volumes of gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Avogadro\'s law.', 3, 'tf'],
        ['The molar mass of CaCO₃ is 100 g/mol.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', '40 + 12 + 48.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['The empirical formula of ethene, C₂H₄, is CH₂.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Ratio 1 : 2.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['One mole of any gas occupies 22.4 dm³ at room temperature and pressure.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', '22.4 dm³ is at s.t.p.; about 24 dm³ at r.t.p.', 3, 'tf'],
        ['The molar mass of iron(III) oxide, Fe₂O₃, is ___ g/mol. (H = 1, C = 12, N = 14, O = 16, Na = 23, Mg = 24, Al = 27, S = 32, Cl = 35.5, K = 39, Ca = 40, Fe = 56, Cu = 64)', ['160'], '', 'Add the relative atomic masses.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['The molar mass of nitrogen, N₂, is ___ g/mol. (H = 1, C = 12, N = 14, O = 16, Na = 23, Mg = 24, Al = 27, S = 32, Cl = 35.5, K = 39, Ca = 40, Fe = 56, Cu = 64)', ['28'], '', 'Add the relative atomic masses.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['The molar mass of sodium chloride, NaCl, is ___ g/mol. (H = 1, C = 12, N = 14, O = 16, Na = 23, Mg = 24, Al = 27, S = 32, Cl = 35.5, K = 39, Ca = 40, Fe = 56, Cu = 64)', ['58.5'], '', 'Add the relative atomic masses.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['The molar mass of chlorine, Cl₂, is ___ g/mol. (H = 1, C = 12, N = 14, O = 16, Na = 23, Mg = 24, Al = 27, S = 32, Cl = 35.5, K = 39, Ca = 40, Fe = 56, Cu = 64)', ['71'], '', 'Add the relative atomic masses.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['The molar mass of copper(II) sulphate, CuSO₄, is ___ g/mol. (H = 1, C = 12, N = 14, O = 16, Na = 23, Mg = 24, Al = 27, S = 32, Cl = 35.5, K = 39, Ca = 40, Fe = 56, Cu = 64)', ['160'], '', 'Add the relative atomic masses.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['The molar mass of potassium hydroxide, KOH, is ___ g/mol. (H = 1, C = 12, N = 14, O = 16, Na = 23, Mg = 24, Al = 27, S = 32, Cl = 35.5, K = 39, Ca = 40, Fe = 56, Cu = 64)', ['56'], '', 'Add the relative atomic masses.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['A compound contains 82.8% carbon, 17.2% hydrogen by mass. Its relative molecular mass is 58. (C = 12, H = 1, O = 16) (a) Find its empirical formula. (b) Find its molecular formula.', ['Divide by Ar', 'Simplest ratio', 'Empirical C₂H₅', 'n = 2', 'Molecular C₄H₁₀'], '(a) Divide by Ar: C = 82.8 ÷ 12 = 6.9; H = 17.2 ÷ 1 = 17.2. Divide by the smallest and round to whole numbers to get the ratio, giving the empirical formula C₂H₅. (b) The empirical formula mass is 29; 58 ÷ 29 = 2, so the molecular formula is C₄H₁₀.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['A compound contains 40% carbon, 6.7% hydrogen and 53.3% oxygen by mass. Its relative molecular mass is 60. (C = 12, H = 1, O = 16) (a) Find its empirical formula. (b) Find its molecular formula.', ['Divide by Ar', 'Simplest ratio', 'Empirical CH₂O', 'n = 2', 'Molecular C₂H₄O₂'], '(a) Divide by Ar: C = 40 ÷ 12 = 3.33; H = 6.7 ÷ 1 = 6.7; O = 53.3 ÷ 16 = 3.33. Divide by the smallest and round to whole numbers to get the ratio, giving the empirical formula CH₂O. (b) The empirical formula mass is 30; 60 ÷ 30 = 2, so the molecular formula is C₂H₄O₂.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['A compound contains 92.3% carbon, 7.7% hydrogen by mass. Its relative molecular mass is 78. (C = 12, H = 1, O = 16) (a) Find its empirical formula. (b) Find its molecular formula.', ['Divide by Ar', 'Simplest ratio', 'Empirical CH', 'n = 6', 'Molecular C₆H₆'], '(a) Divide by Ar: C = 92.3 ÷ 12 = 7.69; H = 7.7 ÷ 1 = 7.7. Divide by the smallest and round to whole numbers to get the ratio, giving the empirical formula CH. (b) The empirical formula mass is 13; 78 ÷ 13 = 6, so the molecular formula is C₆H₆.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['A compound contains 85.7% carbon, 14.3% hydrogen by mass. Its relative molecular mass is 56. (C = 12, H = 1, O = 16) (a) Find its empirical formula. (b) Find its molecular formula.', ['Divide by Ar', 'Simplest ratio', 'Empirical CH₂', 'n = 4', 'Molecular C₄H₈'], '(a) Divide by Ar: C = 85.7 ÷ 12 = 7.14; H = 14.3 ÷ 1 = 14.3. Divide by the smallest and round to whole numbers to get the ratio, giving the empirical formula CH₂. (b) The empirical formula mass is 14; 56 ÷ 14 = 4, so the molecular formula is C₄H₈.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['A compound contains 40% carbon, 6.7% hydrogen and 53.3% oxygen by mass. Its relative molecular mass is 180. (C = 12, H = 1, O = 16) (a) Find its empirical formula. (b) Find its molecular formula.', ['Divide by Ar', 'Simplest ratio', 'Empirical CH₂O', 'n = 6', 'Molecular C₆H₁₂O₆'], '(a) Divide by Ar: C = 40 ÷ 12 = 3.33; H = 6.7 ÷ 1 = 6.7; O = 53.3 ÷ 16 = 3.33. Divide by the smallest and round to whole numbers to get the ratio, giving the empirical formula CH₂O. (b) The empirical formula mass is 30; 180 ÷ 30 = 6, so the molecular formula is C₆H₁₂O₆.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
    ],
    'SHS 1: Chemical equations and stoichiometry' => [
        ['A balanced chemical equation obeys the law of', ['constant composition only', 'multiple proportions', 'definite volumes', 'conservation of mass'], 'D', 'Atoms are neither created nor destroyed.', 1],
        ['Balance: __H₂ + __O₂ → __H₂O', ['2, 1, 2', '1, 1, 1', '1, 2, 2', '2, 2, 1'], 'A', '2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O.', 2],
        ['Balance: __Na + __Cl₂ → __NaCl', ['1, 1, 1', '2, 1, 2', '1, 2, 2', '2, 2, 1'], 'B', '2Na + Cl₂ → 2NaCl.', 2],
        ['Balance: __Mg + __O₂ → __MgO', ['1, 1, 1', '1, 2, 1', '2, 1, 2', '2, 2, 2'], 'C', '2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO.', 2],
        ['Balance: __CH₄ + __O₂ → __CO₂ + __H₂O', ['1, 1, 1, 1', '2, 1, 2, 1', '1, 3, 1, 2', '1, 2, 1, 2'], 'D', 'Count O atoms: 2 + 2.', 3],
        ['Balance: __Al + __O₂ → __Al₂O₃', ['4, 3, 2', '2, 3, 1', '4, 2, 2', '1, 1, 1'], 'A', '4Al + 3O₂ → 2Al₂O₃.', 4],
        ['The state symbol (aq) means', ['a pure liquid', 'dissolved in water', 'a gas', 'a solid'], 'B', 'Aqueous.', 1],
        ['The reactant that is completely used up and limits the amount of product is the', ['excess reagent', 'catalyst', 'limiting reagent', 'solvent'], 'C', 'The other reactant is in excess.', 3],
        ['Percentage yield is', ['theoretical yield ÷ actual yield × 100', 'mass of reactant ÷ mass of product', 'moles × molar mass', 'actual yield ÷ theoretical yield × 100'], 'D', 'Usually below 100%.', 3],
        ['In the ionic equation for the neutralisation of HCl by NaOH, the spectator ions are', ['Na⁺ and Cl⁻', 'H⁺ and OH⁻', 'H⁺ and Cl⁻', 'Na⁺ and OH⁻'], 'A', 'H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O.', 4],
        ['What mass of calcium oxide is formed when 20 g of calcium carbonate is heated completely? CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ (Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16)', ['8.8 g', '11.2 g', '20 g', '35.71 g'], 'B', '20 g ÷ 100 = 0.2 mol; × 56.', 3],
        ['What mass of calcium oxide is formed when 50 g of calcium carbonate is heated completely? CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ (Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16)', ['22 g', '50 g', '28 g', '89.29 g'], 'C', '50 g ÷ 100 = 0.5 mol; × 56.', 3],
        ['What mass of calcium oxide is formed when 5 g of calcium carbonate is heated completely? CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ (Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16)', ['2.2 g', '5 g', '8.93 g', '2.8 g'], 'D', '5 g ÷ 100 = 0.05 mol; × 56.', 3],
        ['What mass of magnesium oxide is produced when 1.2 g of magnesium burns completely? 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO (Mg = 24, O = 16)', ['2 g', '1 g', '4 g', '1.2 g'], 'A', '1.2 ÷ 24 mol of Mg gives the same moles of MgO; × 40.', 3],
        ['What volume of hydrogen at s.t.p. is produced when 13 g of zinc reacts with excess hydrochloric acid? Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂ (Zn = 65, molar volume 22.4 dm³)', ['8.96 dm³', '4.48 dm³', '2.24 dm³', '4.8 dm³'], 'B', '13 ÷ 65 = 0.2 mol H₂; × 22.4.', 4],
        ['What volume of hydrogen at s.t.p. is produced when 3.25 g of zinc reacts with excess hydrochloric acid? Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂ (Zn = 65, molar volume 22.4 dm³)', ['2.24 dm³', '0.56 dm³', '1.12 dm³', '1.2 dm³'], 'C', '3.25 ÷ 65 = 0.05 mol H₂; × 22.4.', 4],
        ['What volume of hydrogen at s.t.p. is produced when 6.5 g of zinc reacts with excess hydrochloric acid? Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂ (Zn = 65, molar volume 22.4 dm³)', ['4.48 dm³', '1.12 dm³', '2.4 dm³', '2.24 dm³'], 'D', '6.5 ÷ 65 = 0.1 mol H₂; × 22.4.', 4],
        ['What mass of magnesium oxide is produced when 4.8 g of magnesium burns completely? 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO (Mg = 24, O = 16)', ['8 g', '4 g', '16 g', '4.8 g'], 'A', '4.8 ÷ 24 mol of Mg gives the same moles of MgO; × 40.', 3],
        ['What mass of calcium oxide is formed when 25 g of calcium carbonate is heated completely? CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ (Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16)', ['11 g', '14 g', '25 g', '44.64 g'], 'B', '25 g ÷ 100 = 0.25 mol; × 56.', 3],
        ['What mass of magnesium oxide is produced when 12 g of magnesium burns completely? 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO (Mg = 24, O = 16)', ['10 g', '40 g', '20 g', '12 g'], 'C', '12 ÷ 24 mol of Mg gives the same moles of MgO; × 40.', 3],
        ['The number of atoms of each element is the same on both sides of a balanced equation.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Conservation of mass.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['You may change subscripts in formulae to balance an equation.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'Only coefficients may be changed.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, 2 mol of hydrogen react with 1 mol of oxygen.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Mole ratio 2 : 1.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Percentage yield can be greater than 100% for a pure, dry product.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'A pure, dry product cannot exceed the theoretical yield.', 3, 'tf'],
        ['Spectator ions take part in the reaction.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'They appear unchanged on both sides.', 3, 'tf'],
        ['The limiting reagent determines the maximum amount of product.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'It runs out first.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['In N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, 0.5 mol of nitrogen reacts completely to give ___ mol of ammonia.', ['1'], '', 'Mole ratio N₂ : NH₃ = 1 : 2.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['In N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, 1 mol of nitrogen reacts completely to give ___ mol of ammonia.', ['2'], '', 'Mole ratio N₂ : NH₃ = 1 : 2.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['In N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, 3 mol of nitrogen reacts completely to give ___ mol of ammonia.', ['6'], '', 'Mole ratio N₂ : NH₃ = 1 : 2.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['In N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, 0.1 mol of nitrogen reacts completely to give ___ mol of ammonia.', ['0.2'], '', 'Mole ratio N₂ : NH₃ = 1 : 2.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['In N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, 2 mol of nitrogen reacts completely to give ___ mol of ammonia.', ['4'], '', 'Mole ratio N₂ : NH₃ = 1 : 2.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['In N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, 0.2 mol of nitrogen reacts completely to give ___ mol of ammonia.', ['0.4'], '', 'Mole ratio N₂ : NH₃ = 1 : 2.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Sodium hydroxide reacts with hydrochloric acid: NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O. A student neutralised 20 g of sodium hydroxide with excess acid and obtained 23.4 g of dry sodium chloride. (Na = 23, O = 16, H = 1, Cl = 35.5) Calculate (a) the moles of NaOH used, (b) the theoretical mass of NaCl, (c) the percentage yield. (d) Give one reason why the yield is less than 100%.', ['Moles NaOH', 'Theoretical mass', 'Percentage yield', 'Reason for loss'], '(a) Mr NaOH = 40, so moles = 20 ÷ 40 = 0.5 mol. (b) Mole ratio 1 : 1, so NaCl = 0.5 mol × 58.5 = 29.25 g. (c) Percentage yield = 23.4 ÷ 29.25 × 100 = 80%. (d) Some product is lost during transfer, filtration or crystallisation, or some remains dissolved in the solution.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Sodium hydroxide reacts with hydrochloric acid: NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O. A student neutralised 4 g of sodium hydroxide with excess acid and obtained 5.27 g of dry sodium chloride. (Na = 23, O = 16, H = 1, Cl = 35.5) Calculate (a) the moles of NaOH used, (b) the theoretical mass of NaCl, (c) the percentage yield. (d) Give one reason why the yield is less than 100%.', ['Moles NaOH', 'Theoretical mass', 'Percentage yield', 'Reason for loss'], '(a) Mr NaOH = 40, so moles = 4 ÷ 40 = 0.1 mol. (b) Mole ratio 1 : 1, so NaCl = 0.1 mol × 58.5 = 5.85 g. (c) Percentage yield = 5.27 ÷ 5.85 × 100 = 90.1%. (d) Some product is lost during transfer, filtration or crystallisation, or some remains dissolved in the solution.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Sodium hydroxide reacts with hydrochloric acid: NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O. A student neutralised 10 g of sodium hydroxide with excess acid and obtained 11.7 g of dry sodium chloride. (Na = 23, O = 16, H = 1, Cl = 35.5) Calculate (a) the moles of NaOH used, (b) the theoretical mass of NaCl, (c) the percentage yield. (d) Give one reason why the yield is less than 100%.', ['Moles NaOH', 'Theoretical mass', 'Percentage yield', 'Reason for loss'], '(a) Mr NaOH = 40, so moles = 10 ÷ 40 = 0.25 mol. (b) Mole ratio 1 : 1, so NaCl = 0.25 mol × 58.5 = 14.63 g. (c) Percentage yield = 11.7 ÷ 14.63 × 100 = 80%. (d) Some product is lost during transfer, filtration or crystallisation, or some remains dissolved in the solution.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Sodium hydroxide reacts with hydrochloric acid: NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O. A student neutralised 4 g of sodium hydroxide with excess acid and obtained 4.68 g of dry sodium chloride. (Na = 23, O = 16, H = 1, Cl = 35.5) Calculate (a) the moles of NaOH used, (b) the theoretical mass of NaCl, (c) the percentage yield. (d) Give one reason why the yield is less than 100%.', ['Moles NaOH', 'Theoretical mass', 'Percentage yield', 'Reason for loss'], '(a) Mr NaOH = 40, so moles = 4 ÷ 40 = 0.1 mol. (b) Mole ratio 1 : 1, so NaCl = 0.1 mol × 58.5 = 5.85 g. (c) Percentage yield = 4.68 ÷ 5.85 × 100 = 80%. (d) Some product is lost during transfer, filtration or crystallisation, or some remains dissolved in the solution.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Sodium hydroxide reacts with hydrochloric acid: NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O. A student neutralised 8 g of sodium hydroxide with excess acid and obtained 10.53 g of dry sodium chloride. (Na = 23, O = 16, H = 1, Cl = 35.5) Calculate (a) the moles of NaOH used, (b) the theoretical mass of NaCl, (c) the percentage yield. (d) Give one reason why the yield is less than 100%.', ['Moles NaOH', 'Theoretical mass', 'Percentage yield', 'Reason for loss'], '(a) Mr NaOH = 40, so moles = 8 ÷ 40 = 0.2 mol. (b) Mole ratio 1 : 1, so NaCl = 0.2 mol × 58.5 = 11.7 g. (c) Percentage yield = 10.53 ÷ 11.7 × 100 = 90%. (d) Some product is lost during transfer, filtration or crystallisation, or some remains dissolved in the solution.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
    ],
    'SHS 1: Gas laws and kinetic theory' => [
        ['Boyle\'s law states that at constant temperature, the volume of a fixed mass of gas is', ['directly proportional to its pressure', 'directly proportional to its temperature', 'independent of pressure', 'inversely proportional to its pressure'], 'D', 'PV = constant.', 2],
        ['Charles\'s law states that at constant pressure, the volume of a fixed mass of gas is', ['directly proportional to its absolute temperature', 'inversely proportional to temperature', 'proportional to pressure', 'constant'], 'A', 'V/T = constant.', 2],
        ['Absolute zero on the Kelvin scale is', ['0 °C', '0 K, which is −273 °C', '273 °C', '100 K'], 'B', 'Lowest possible temperature.', 2],
        ['Standard temperature and pressure (s.t.p.) means', ['298 K and 760 mmHg', '0 K and 1 atm', '273 K and 760 mmHg', '373 K and 1 atm'], 'C', '0 °C and 1 atm.', 2],
        ['Graham\'s law states that the rate of diffusion of a gas is', ['directly proportional to its density', 'proportional to its mass', 'independent of mass', 'inversely proportional to the square root of its density'], 'D', 'Lighter gases diffuse faster.', 4],
        ['According to the kinetic theory, gas pressure is caused by', ['gas molecules colliding with the walls of the container', 'gas molecules attracting each other', 'the weight of the container', 'molecules standing still'], 'A', 'Momentum change on impact.', 2],
        ['Dalton\'s law of partial pressures states that the total pressure of a gas mixture is', ['the product of the partial pressures', 'the sum of the partial pressures of the gases', 'the pressure of the heaviest gas', 'always 1 atm'], 'B', 'P = p₁ + p₂ + …', 4],
