        ['Chlorine is more electronegative than iodine.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Electronegativity falls down group 17.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Mendeleev left gaps in his table for undiscovered elements.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'He predicted their properties.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Lithium (Z = 3) is in period ___ of the periodic table.', ['2'], '', 'It has 2 occupied shells.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Silicon (Z = 14) is in period ___ of the periodic table.', ['3'], '', 'It has 3 occupied shells.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Argon (Z = 18) is in period ___ of the periodic table.', ['3'], '', 'It has 3 occupied shells.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Oxygen (Z = 8) is in period ___ of the periodic table.', ['2'], '', 'It has 2 occupied shells.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Aluminium (Z = 13) is in period ___ of the periodic table.', ['3'], '', 'It has 3 occupied shells.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Sodium (Z = 11) is in period ___ of the periodic table.', ['3'], '', 'It has 3 occupied shells.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Explain the trend in atomic radius (a) across period 3 from sodium to chlorine, (b) down group 1 from lithium to potassium.', ['Decrease across', 'Increasing nuclear charge, same shell', 'Increase down', 'Extra shells and shielding'], '(a) Atomic radius decreases from Na to Cl. The number of protons (nuclear charge) increases while electrons are added to the same shell, so shielding hardly changes. The outer electrons are pulled closer to the nucleus. (b) Atomic radius increases from Li to K because a new electron shell is added each time, the outer electrons are further from the nucleus and inner shells shield them more.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Define first ionisation energy. Explain why the first ionisation energy of sodium is lower than that of magnesium, and why that of potassium is lower than that of sodium.', ['Definition with gaseous atoms', 'Mg greater nuclear charge', 'K outer electron further', 'Shielding'], 'First ionisation energy is the energy needed to remove one mole of electrons from one mole of gaseous atoms to form one mole of gaseous 1+ ions. Magnesium has a greater nuclear charge than sodium with its outer electrons in the same shell, so they are held more strongly. Potassium\'s outer electron is in the fourth shell, further from the nucleus and better shielded than sodium\'s third-shell electron, so it is removed more easily.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['An element X has atomic number 12. (a) Write its electron configuration. (b) State its group and period. (c) Is it a metal or non-metal? Give a reason. (d) Write the formula of its oxide and chloride.', ['Configuration', 'Group 2 period 3', 'Metal with reason', 'XO and XCl₂'], '(a) 2, 8, 2 (1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s²). (b) Group 2, period 3. (c) A metal, because it has 2 valence electrons which it loses to form X²⁺ ions. (d) Oxide XO; chloride XCl₂.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 2, 'theory', 6],
        ['Describe how Mendeleev arranged the elements and give two ways in which the modern periodic table differs from his.', ['Atomic mass order', 'Similar properties in columns', 'Gaps and predictions', 'Atomic number', 'Noble gases or d-block'], 'Mendeleev arranged the known elements in order of increasing atomic mass and placed elements with similar properties in the same vertical columns. He left gaps for undiscovered elements and predicted their properties (e.g. eka-silicon, germanium). The modern table arranges elements by atomic number, not atomic mass; it includes the noble gases (group 18) which were unknown to him; and it separates the transition elements into a d-block.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 5],
        ['Compare the elements in period 3 in terms of (a) the type of oxide they form and (b) their electrical conductivity. Use sodium, aluminium and sulphur as examples.', ['Na₂O basic', 'Al₂O₃ amphoteric', 'SO₂ acidic', 'Metals conduct, sulphur does not'], '(a) Sodium forms Na₂O, a basic oxide that dissolves to give an alkali. Aluminium forms Al₂O₃, an amphoteric oxide that reacts with both acids and bases. Sulphur forms SO₂ and SO₃, acidic oxides that dissolve to give acids. (b) Sodium and aluminium are metals with delocalised electrons, so they conduct electricity well; sulphur is a non-metal with no free electrons, so it does not conduct.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
    ],
    'SHS 1: Ionic, covalent and metallic bonding' => [
        ['An ionic bond is formed by', ['sharing of electrons between two non-metals', 'a sea of delocalised electrons', 'the transfer of electrons from a metal to a non-metal', 'sharing of protons'], 'C', 'Oppositely charged ions attract.', 1],
        ['A covalent bond is formed by', ['transferring electrons', 'attraction between ions', 'sharing neutrons', 'sharing a pair of electrons between atoms'], 'D', 'Usually between non-metals.', 1],
        ['A dative (coordinate) covalent bond is one in which', ['both shared electrons come from the same atom', 'electrons are transferred completely', 'no electrons are shared', 'three electrons are shared'], 'A', 'For example in NH₄⁺.', 3],
        ['Metallic bonding is the attraction between', ['positive and negative ions', 'positive metal ions and a sea of delocalised electrons', 'shared pairs of electrons', 'molecules'], 'B', 'It explains conductivity.', 2],
        ['The number of covalent bonds in a nitrogen molecule, N₂, is', ['1', '2', '3 (a triple bond)', '4'], 'C', 'Remember: N≡N.', 2],
        ['The ammonium ion, NH₄⁺, contains', ['four ionic bonds', 'only dative bonds', 'one ionic bond', 'three normal covalent bonds and one dative bond'], 'D', 'H⁺ accepts the lone pair of NH₃.', 4],
        ['Which compound is ionic?', ['magnesium oxide', 'methane', 'carbon dioxide', 'hydrogen chloride gas'], 'A', 'Metal + non-metal.', 1],
        ['Which substance is a giant covalent structure?', ['sodium chloride', 'diamond', 'iodine', 'water'], 'B', 'Each C is bonded to four others.', 2],
        ['Graphite conducts electricity because', ['it contains ions', 'it is a metal', 'each carbon has one delocalised electron', 'it has a dative bond'], 'C', 'Three bonds per carbon; one free electron.', 3],
        ['The formula of the compound formed between aluminium and oxygen is', ['AlO', 'Al₃O₂', 'AlO₃', 'Al₂O₃'], 'D', 'Al³⁺ and O²⁻.', 2],
        ['The formula of calcium chloride is', ['CaCl₂', 'CaCl', 'Ca₂Cl', 'CaCl₃'], 'A', 'Ca²⁺ and two Cl⁻.', 1],
        ['The formula of sodium sulphate is', ['NaSO₄', 'Na₂SO₄', 'Na(SO₄)₂', 'Na₃SO₄'], 'B', 'Two Na⁺ for one SO₄²⁻.', 2],
        ['Metals are malleable because', ['they have no electrons', 'they are covalent', 'layers of ions can slide over each other without breaking the metallic bond', 'their ions repel strongly'], 'C', 'The electron sea keeps them bonded.', 3],
        ['A polar covalent bond forms between atoms that', ['are identical', 'are both metals', 'have no electrons', 'have different electronegativities'], 'D', 'For example H–Cl.', 3],
        ['The bond in a chlorine molecule is', ['non-polar covalent', 'ionic', 'polar covalent', 'metallic'], 'A', 'Identical atoms share equally.', 2],
        ['In a dot-and-cross diagram of water, the oxygen atom has', ['four bonding pairs', 'two bonding pairs and two lone pairs', 'one lone pair', 'no lone pairs'], 'B', 'Eight electrons in its outer shell.', 3],
        ['Covalent molecular substances usually have low melting points because', ['their covalent bonds are weak', 'they contain ions', 'only weak intermolecular forces must be overcome', 'they are metals'], 'C', 'The covalent bonds are not broken on melting.', 3],
        ['Ionic compounds conduct electricity in the solid state.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'The ions are fixed in the lattice.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Diamond does not conduct electricity.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'All four outer electrons are used in bonding.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['A double bond contains two shared pairs of electrons.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'For example O=O.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['Metals conduct electricity because of delocalised electrons.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'They move through the lattice.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['Hydrogen chloride gas is an ionic compound.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'It is a polar covalent molecule.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['A carbon monoxide molecule contains a dative bond.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'C≡O includes one dative bond from oxygen.', 5, 'tf'],
        ['The bond formed when both shared electrons come from one atom is a ___ bond.', ['dative', 'coordinate', 'dative covalent', 'co-ordinate'], '', 'Also called a coordinate bond.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['Magnesium loses ___ electrons to form the Mg²⁺ ion.', ['2', 'two'], '', 'It has two valence electrons.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['The formula of potassium oxide is ___.', ['K₂O', 'K2O'], '', 'K⁺ and O²⁻.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['The formula of magnesium nitride is ___.', ['Mg₃N₂', 'Mg3N2'], '', 'Mg²⁺ and N³⁻.', 4, 'fill'],
        ['The type of bonding in copper metal is ___ bonding.', ['metallic'], '', 'Ions in a sea of electrons.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['In a nitrogen molecule the atoms are joined by a ___ bond.', ['triple', 'triple covalent'], '', 'Remember: N≡N.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Using dot-and-cross diagrams, show the formation of (a) sodium chloride, (b) a molecule of water. (c) Explain why sodium chloride has a much higher melting point than water.', ['NaCl transfer diagram', 'Ion charges', 'H₂O shared pairs', 'Lone pairs', 'Giant lattice vs weak intermolecular forces'], '(a) Sodium (2, 8, 1) transfers its one outer electron to chlorine (2, 8, 7), giving Na⁺ (2, 8) and Cl⁻ (2, 8, 8); diagram shows square brackets and charges. (b) Oxygen shares one electron pair with each of two hydrogen atoms, giving two bonding pairs and two lone pairs on oxygen. (c) Sodium chloride is a giant ionic lattice with strong electrostatic attractions between ions in all directions, needing much energy to break. Water is a simple molecule; only weak intermolecular forces (hydrogen bonds) must be overcome to melt it, not the covalent bonds.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Compare the structure and properties of diamond and graphite. Include hardness, electrical conductivity and one use of each.', ['Diamond 4 bonds tetrahedral', 'Hard, non-conductor', 'Graphite layers 3 bonds', 'Soft and conducts', 'Uses'], 'Both are giant covalent forms of carbon. In diamond each carbon is bonded to four others in a rigid tetrahedral network; it is very hard, does not conduct electricity (no free electrons) and is used in cutting and drilling tools and jewellery. In graphite each carbon is bonded to three others in flat hexagonal layers held by weak forces; the layers slide, so it is soft and slippery, and each carbon has one delocalised electron, so it conducts electricity. It is used as a lubricant, in pencils and as electrodes.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain metallic bonding and use it to account for three properties of metals.', ['Definition', 'Conductivity', 'Malleability', 'High melting point or thermal conductivity'], 'Metallic bonding is the electrostatic attraction between a lattice of positive metal ions and a sea of delocalised valence electrons. Properties: good electrical conductivity, because delocalised electrons carry charge through the metal; malleability and ductility, because layers of ions slide over each other while the electron sea keeps them bonded; high melting points, because the attraction between ions and electrons is strong; good thermal conductivity, because free electrons transfer energy.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain, with an example, the formation of a dative covalent bond. Show how the ammonium ion is formed.', ['Definition', 'Lone pair donor', 'Empty orbital acceptor', 'NH₃ + H⁺ → NH₄⁺', 'Bonds identical'], 'A dative covalent bond forms when one atom donates both electrons of the shared pair to another atom that has an empty orbital. In ammonia, the nitrogen atom has a lone pair. A hydrogen ion, H⁺, has no electrons. The lone pair on nitrogen is donated to H⁺: NH₃ + H⁺ → NH₄⁺. Once formed, all four N–H bonds in the ammonium ion are identical.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 5],
        ['Using the charges on the ions, work out the formulae of (a) calcium nitrate, (b) iron(III) oxide, (c) ammonium sulphate, (d) aluminium chloride, (e) sodium carbonate. Show your working for two of them.', ['Ca(NO₃)₂', 'Fe₂O₃', '(NH₄)₂SO₄', 'AlCl₃', 'Na₂CO₃'], '(a) Ca²⁺ and NO₃⁻ give Ca(NO₃)₂. (b) Fe³⁺ and O²⁻ give Fe₂O₃ (the charges cross over: 2 × 3+ = 3 × 2−). (c) NH₄⁺ and SO₄²⁻ give (NH₄)₂SO₄. (d) Al³⁺ and Cl⁻ give AlCl₃. (e) Na⁺ and CO₃²⁻ give Na₂CO₃. In each, the total positive charge equals the total negative charge.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 5],
    ],
