        ['Ammonia has ___ lone pair(s) of electrons on nitrogen.', ['1', 'one'], '', 'Five valence electrons, three used in bonding.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['A molecule with six bonding pairs and no lone pairs has a(n) ___ shape.', ['octahedral'], '', 'For example SF₆.', 4, 'fill'],
        ['Use VSEPR theory to explain the shapes and bond angles of CH₄, NH₃ and H₂O.', ['VSEPR principle', 'CH₄ tetrahedral 109.5°', 'NH₃ pyramidal 107°', 'H₂O bent 104.5°', 'Lone pair effect'], 'Electron pairs around the central atom repel and get as far apart as possible; lone pairs repel more than bonding pairs. CH₄: four bonding pairs, no lone pairs, so tetrahedral with 109.5°. NH₃: four electron pairs (three bonding, one lone), so the pairs are tetrahedral but the shape is trigonal pyramidal; the lone pair squeezes the angle to about 107°. H₂O: two bonding and two lone pairs, so the shape is bent and the angle is reduced further to about 104.5°.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['(a) Explain what is meant by hydrogen bonding. (b) Explain why water has a higher boiling point than hydrogen sulphide. (c) Explain why ice floats on water.', ['Definition with N, O, F', 'Water H-bonds, H₂S does not', 'Higher boiling point', 'Open lattice in ice', 'Lower density'], '(a) Hydrogen bonding is a strong intermolecular attraction between a hydrogen atom bonded to N, O or F in one molecule and a lone pair on a N, O or F atom of another molecule. (b) Water molecules form hydrogen bonds with each other; H₂S cannot because S is not electronegative enough. More energy is needed to separate water molecules, so water boils at 100 °C while H₂S is a gas at room temperature. (c) In ice, hydrogen bonds hold the molecules in an open hexagonal lattice with more space than in liquid water, so ice is less dense and floats.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 7],
        ['Explain the difference between intramolecular and intermolecular forces. Explain why the boiling points of the noble gases increase from helium to xenon.', ['Intramolecular = bonds within', 'Intermolecular = between molecules', 'Only intermolecular broken on boiling', 'More electrons down group', 'Stronger van der Waals'], 'Intramolecular forces are the bonds within a molecule (covalent bonds), and they are strong. Intermolecular forces act between separate molecules, such as van der Waals forces, dipole–dipole forces and hydrogen bonds, and they are much weaker. When a simple molecular substance boils, only the intermolecular forces are overcome. Noble gases exist as single atoms held together only by van der Waals forces. From He to Xe the atoms have more electrons, so larger temporary dipoles form, the van der Waals forces get stronger and more energy is needed to separate the atoms.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['Predict the shape and bond angle of each molecule and give a reason: (a) BeCl₂, (b) BF₃, (c) SiH₄, (d) PH₃.', ['BeCl₂ linear', 'BF₃ trigonal planar', 'SiH₄ tetrahedral', 'PH₃ pyramidal'], '(a) BeCl₂: two bonding pairs, no lone pairs, so linear with 180°. (b) BF₃: three bonding pairs, no lone pairs, so trigonal planar with 120°. (c) SiH₄: four bonding pairs, so tetrahedral with 109.5°. (d) PH₃: three bonding pairs and one lone pair, so trigonal pyramidal with an angle less than 109.5° (about 93° to 107°).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain, with examples, the terms polar molecule and non-polar molecule. Why is tetrachloromethane, CCl₄, non-polar even though the C–Cl bond is polar?', ['Polar definition and example', 'Non-polar definition and example', 'Symmetry cancels dipoles', 'CCl₄ tetrahedral'], 'A polar molecule has a permanent dipole because its bonds are polar and the molecule is not symmetrical, so the centres of positive and negative charge do not coincide, for example HCl and H₂O. A non-polar molecule has no overall dipole, either because its bonds are non-polar (Cl₂, H₂) or because polar bonds are arranged symmetrically and cancel (CO₂). CCl₄ is tetrahedral, so the four C–Cl bond dipoles point symmetrically outward and cancel, giving no overall dipole.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
    ],
    'SHS 1: Introduction to organic chemistry: naming and isomerism' => [
        ['Organic chemistry is the study of compounds of', ['nitrogen', 'metals', 'oxygen', 'carbon'], 'D', 'Except a few such as CO₂ and carbonates.', 1],
        ['A homologous series is a family of compounds that', ['has the same general formula and functional group', 'has the same molecular mass', 'all have the same boiling point', 'contains only metals'], 'A', 'Members differ by CH₂.', 2],
        ['Successive members of a homologous series differ by', ['a CH₃ group', 'a CH₂ group', 'one carbon atom only', 'an oxygen atom'], 'B', 'Mass 14.', 2],
        ['Structural isomers have', ['different molecular formulae', 'the same structure', 'the same molecular formula but different structural formulae', 'different numbers of carbon atoms'], 'C', 'For example butane and 2-methylpropane.', 2],
        ['The number of structural isomers of C₄H₁₀ is', ['1', '3', '4', '2'], 'D', 'Butane and 2-methylpropane.', 3],
        ['The number of structural isomers of C₅H₁₂ is', ['3', '2', '4', '5'], 'A', 'Pentane, 2-methylbutane, 2,2-dimethylpropane.', 4],
        ['The IUPAC name of CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₃ is', ['butane', '2-methylpropane', '1-methylpropane', 'isobutene'], 'B', 'Longest chain has three carbons.', 3],
        ['Cis-trans (geometric) isomerism occurs in alkenes because', ['they have a ring', 'they have many hydrogens', 'there is no free rotation about the C=C double bond', 'they are saturated'], 'C', 'Each C of the double bond needs two different groups.', 4],
        ['What is the molecular formula of ethene?', ['C₂H₆', 'C₂H₂', 'CH₄', 'C₂H₄'], 'D', 'Alkenes: CₙH₂ₙ with n = 2.', 2],
        ['What is the molecular formula of heptene?', ['C₇H₁₄', 'C₇H₁₆', 'C₇H₁₂', 'C₆H₁₄'], 'A', 'Alkenes: CₙH₂ₙ with n = 7.', 2],
        ['What is the molecular formula of hexene?', ['C₆H₁₄', 'C₆H₁₂', 'C₆H₁₀', 'C₅H₁₂'], 'B', 'Alkenes: CₙH₂ₙ with n = 6.', 2],
        ['What is the molecular formula of butene?', ['C₄H₁₀', 'C₄H₆', 'C₄H₈', 'C₃H₈'], 'C', 'Alkenes: CₙH₂ₙ with n = 4.', 2],
        ['What is the molecular formula of pentene?', ['C₅H₁₂', 'C₅H₈', 'C₄H₁₀', 'C₅H₁₀'], 'D', 'Alkenes: CₙH₂ₙ with n = 5.', 2],
        ['What is the molecular formula of heptane?', ['C₇H₁₆', 'C₇H₁₄', 'C₇H₁₂', 'C₈H₁₆'], 'A', 'Alkanes: CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ with n = 7.', 2],
        ['What is the molecular formula of nonane?', ['C₉H₁₈', 'C₉H₂₀', 'C₉H₁₆', 'C₁₀H₂₀'], 'B', 'Alkanes: CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ with n = 9.', 2],
        ['What is the molecular formula of propane?', ['C₃H₆', 'C₃H₄', 'C₃H₈', 'C₄H₈'], 'C', 'Alkanes: CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ with n = 3.', 2],
        ['Members of a homologous series show a gradual change in physical properties.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'For example boiling point rises with chain length.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Alkanes are unsaturated hydrocarbons.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'They have only single bonds and are saturated.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['Butane and 2-methylpropane are structural isomers.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Both are C₄H₁₀.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Ethanol and dimethyl ether are isomers.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Both are C₂H₆O.', 4, 'tf'],
        ['But-2-ene shows cis-trans isomerism.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Each carbon of C=C has H and CH₃.', 4, 'tf'],
        ['The prefix "prop" means two carbon atoms.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'Prop means three; eth means two.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['An unbranched alkane with 7 carbon atoms is named ___.', ['heptane'], '', 'Prefix for 7 carbons + "ane".', 2, 'fill'],
        ['An unbranched alkane with 3 carbon atoms is named ___.', ['propane'], '', 'Prefix for 3 carbons + "ane".', 2, 'fill'],
        ['An unbranched alkane with 1 carbon atom is named ___.', ['methane'], '', 'Prefix for 1 carbon + "ane".', 2, 'fill'],
        ['An unbranched alkane with 8 carbon atoms is named ___.', ['octane'], '', 'Prefix for 8 carbons + "ane".', 2, 'fill'],
