        ['An unbranched alkane with 4 carbon atoms is named ___.', ['butane'], '', 'Prefix for 4 carbons + "ane".', 2, 'fill'],
        ['(a) Define homologous series. (b) State four characteristics of a homologous series. (c) Name and write the formulae of the first three members of the alkane series.', ['Definition', 'Four characteristics', 'CH₄', 'C₂H₆', 'C₃H₈'], '(a) A homologous series is a family of organic compounds with the same functional group and general formula, in which each member differs from the next by a CH₂ group. (b) Members have the same general formula; they differ by CH₂; they have similar chemical properties; their physical properties (such as boiling point) change gradually; they can be prepared by similar methods. (c) Methane CH₄, ethane C₂H₆, propane C₃H₈.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Draw the structural formulae and give the IUPAC names of all the isomers of C₅H₁₂.', ['Pentane', '2-methylbutane', '2,2-dimethylpropane', 'Structures correct'], 'Pentane: CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃. 2-methylbutane: CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃. 2,2-dimethylpropane: C(CH₃)₄. (Each drawn showing all C–C and C–H bonds.) They are structural (chain) isomers with the same molecular formula but different carbon skeletons.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain why but-2-ene has cis and trans isomers but but-1-ene does not. Draw the two isomers of but-2-ene.', ['No rotation about C=C', 'Two different groups needed', 'cis structure', 'trans structure', 'But-1-ene has CH₂='], 'Cis-trans isomerism arises because there is no free rotation about a C=C double bond, and each carbon of the double bond carries two different groups. In but-2-ene, CH₃CH=CHCH₃, each double-bond carbon has an H and a CH₃: in cis-but-2-ene both CH₃ groups are on the same side; in trans-but-2-ene they are on opposite sides. In but-1-ene, CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃, the first carbon has two identical H atoms, so no geometric isomers exist.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 6],
        ['Name the functional group and give one example (name and formula) of each: (a) alkenes, (b) alkanols, (c) alkanoic acids, (d) esters, (e) alkynes.', ['Alkene', 'Alkanol', 'Alkanoic acid', 'Ester', 'Alkyne'], '(a) C=C double bond; ethene, C₂H₄. (b) Hydroxyl –OH; ethanol, C₂H₅OH. (c) Carboxyl –COOH; ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH. (d) Ester –COO–; ethyl ethanoate, CH₃COOC₂H₅. (e) C≡C triple bond; ethyne, C₂H₂.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 2, 'theory', 5],
        ['Explain why carbon forms so many compounds. Refer to catenation, tetravalency and multiple bonding.', ['Four bonds', 'Catenation', 'Chains, branches, rings', 'Multiple bonds', 'Isomerism'], 'Carbon has four valence electrons and forms four covalent bonds (tetravalency), so it can bond to many other atoms. It can bond strongly to other carbon atoms to form long chains, branched chains and rings (catenation), because C–C bonds are strong. It can also form double and triple bonds with itself and other atoms. Together with isomerism, these allow millions of different organic compounds.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 5],
    ],
