        ['Distinguish between temporary and permanent hardness of water. For each, name one cause and one method of removal, with an equation where possible.', ['Temporary cause', 'Boiling equation', 'Permanent cause', 'Washing soda equation', 'Ion exchange or distillation'], 'Temporary hardness is caused by dissolved calcium or magnesium hydrogencarbonate and is removed by boiling: Ca(HCO₃)₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O + CO₂. Permanent hardness is caused by dissolved calcium or magnesium sulphate or chloride and is not removed by boiling. It is removed by adding washing soda: CaSO₄ + Na₂CO₃ → CaCO₃ + Na₂SO₄, or by ion exchange or distillation.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Describe the stages in the treatment of water for a town supply.', ['Screening', 'Coagulation with alum', 'Sedimentation', 'Filtration', 'Chlorination'], 'Water from a river or dam is passed through screens to remove large objects. Alum (and lime) is added so that fine particles coagulate (flocculation) and settle in sedimentation tanks. The water is filtered through beds of sand and gravel to remove remaining particles. Chlorine (or ozone) is added to kill bacteria. Sometimes fluoride is added and the pH is adjusted before the water is stored and pumped to homes.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['(a) Explain the terms solute, solvent and saturated solution. (b) 60 g of a salt saturates 150 g of water at 40 °C. Calculate its solubility in g per 100 g of water. (c) Describe how you would determine the solubility of a salt at a given temperature.', ['Definitions', '40 g/100 g', 'Saturated solution at T', 'Evaporate weighed sample', 'Calculation'], '(a) The solute is the substance that dissolves; the solvent is the liquid that dissolves it; a saturated solution contains the maximum amount of solute that can dissolve at that temperature. (b) Solubility = 60 ÷ 150 × 100 = 40 g per 100 g of water. (c) Make a saturated solution at the temperature with excess solid, filter, weigh an evaporating dish, add some filtrate and reweigh, evaporate to dryness and weigh again; solubility = mass of solid ÷ mass of water × 100.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain the terms deliquescent, efflorescent and hygroscopic, giving one example of each.', ['Deliquescent and example', 'Efflorescent and example', 'Hygroscopic and example'], 'A deliquescent substance absorbs water vapour from the air and dissolves in it to form a solution, for example sodium hydroxide or calcium chloride. An efflorescent substance loses some or all of its water of crystallisation to the air, becoming powdery, for example washing soda, Na₂CO₃·10H₂O. A hygroscopic substance absorbs water vapour from the air without forming a solution, for example concentrated sulphuric acid or calcium oxide.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['State three advantages and three disadvantages of hard water.', ['Three advantages', 'Scum wastes soap', 'Scale in kettles and pipes', 'Third disadvantage'], 'Advantages: it contains calcium needed for strong bones and teeth; it has a pleasant taste; it reduces the dissolving of lead from pipes; it is good for brewing. Disadvantages: it wastes soap by forming scum; it forms scale (fur) in kettles, boilers and pipes, wasting energy and blocking pipes; it can stain baths and sinks; it makes clothes feel rough.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 2, 'theory', 6],
    ],
