        ['Heat absorbed when 200 g of water warms by 2 °C (c = 4.2 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹) is ___ J.', ['1,680', '1680'], '', 'q = mcΔT.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Heat absorbed when 50 g of water warms by 4 °C (c = 4.2 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹) is ___ J.', ['840'], '', 'q = mcΔT.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['In an experiment, 0.92 g of ethanol (C₂H₅OH, M = 46) was burned to heat 100 g of water. The temperature of the water rose by 26 °C. (c = 4.2 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹) (a) Calculate the heat absorbed by the water. (b) Calculate the moles of ethanol burned. (c) Calculate the enthalpy of combustion of ethanol. (d) Give two reasons why the value is less than the data-book value of −1,367 kJ/mol.', ['q = mcΔT', 'Moles ethanol', 'ΔH value negative', 'Two reasons'], '(a) q = 100 × 4.2 × 26 = 10,920 J = 10.92 kJ. (b) n = 0.92 ÷ 46 = 0.02 mol. (c) ΔH = −10.92 ÷ 0.02 = −546 kJ/mol. (d) Heat is lost to the surroundings and the container; combustion may be incomplete; some ethanol evaporates; conditions are not standard.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['In an experiment, 0.92 g of ethanol (C₂H₅OH, M = 46) was burned to heat 200 g of water. The temperature of the water rose by 13 °C. (c = 4.2 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹) (a) Calculate the heat absorbed by the water. (b) Calculate the moles of ethanol burned. (c) Calculate the enthalpy of combustion of ethanol. (d) Give two reasons why the value is less than the data-book value of −1,367 kJ/mol.', ['q = mcΔT', 'Moles ethanol', 'ΔH value negative', 'Two reasons'], '(a) q = 200 × 4.2 × 13 = 10,920 J = 10.92 kJ. (b) n = 0.92 ÷ 46 = 0.02 mol. (c) ΔH = −10.92 ÷ 0.02 = −546 kJ/mol. (d) Heat is lost to the surroundings and the container; combustion may be incomplete; some ethanol evaporates; conditions are not standard.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['In an experiment, 0.46 g of ethanol (C₂H₅OH, M = 46) was burned to heat 200 g of water. The temperature of the water rose by 6.5 °C. (c = 4.2 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹) (a) Calculate the heat absorbed by the water. (b) Calculate the moles of ethanol burned. (c) Calculate the enthalpy of combustion of ethanol. (d) Give two reasons why the value is less than the data-book value of −1,367 kJ/mol.', ['q = mcΔT', 'Moles ethanol', 'ΔH value negative', 'Two reasons'], '(a) q = 200 × 4.2 × 6.5 = 5,460 J = 5.46 kJ. (b) n = 0.46 ÷ 46 = 0.01 mol. (c) ΔH = −5.46 ÷ 0.01 = −546 kJ/mol. (d) Heat is lost to the surroundings and the container; combustion may be incomplete; some ethanol evaporates; conditions are not standard.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['In an experiment, 0.23 g of ethanol (C₂H₅OH, M = 46) was burned to heat 250 g of water. The temperature of the water rose by 2.6 °C. (c = 4.2 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹) (a) Calculate the heat absorbed by the water. (b) Calculate the moles of ethanol burned. (c) Calculate the enthalpy of combustion of ethanol. (d) Give two reasons why the value is less than the data-book value of −1,367 kJ/mol.', ['q = mcΔT', 'Moles ethanol', 'ΔH value negative', 'Two reasons'], '(a) q = 250 × 4.2 × 2.6 = 2,730 J = 2.73 kJ. (b) n = 0.23 ÷ 46 = 0.005 mol. (c) ΔH = −2.73 ÷ 0.005 = −546 kJ/mol. (d) Heat is lost to the surroundings and the container; combustion may be incomplete; some ethanol evaporates; conditions are not standard.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['In an experiment, 0.92 g of ethanol (C₂H₅OH, M = 46) was burned to heat 250 g of water. The temperature of the water rose by 10.4 °C. (c = 4.2 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹) (a) Calculate the heat absorbed by the water. (b) Calculate the moles of ethanol burned. (c) Calculate the enthalpy of combustion of ethanol. (d) Give two reasons why the value is less than the data-book value of −1,367 kJ/mol.', ['q = mcΔT', 'Moles ethanol', 'ΔH value negative', 'Two reasons'], '(a) q = 250 × 4.2 × 10.4 = 10,920 J = 10.92 kJ. (b) n = 0.92 ÷ 46 = 0.02 mol. (c) ΔH = −10.92 ÷ 0.02 = −546 kJ/mol. (d) Heat is lost to the surroundings and the container; combustion may be incomplete; some ethanol evaporates; conditions are not standard.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
    ],
    'SHS 2: Rates of reaction and collision theory' => [
        ['According to collision theory, a reaction occurs when particles collide', ['at any speed', 'only in the gas phase', 'with energy at least equal to the activation energy and correct orientation', 'without any energy'], 'C', 'Effective collisions.', 2],
        ['Increasing the temperature increases the rate mainly because', ['the activation energy increases', 'particles become larger', 'the catalyst is used up', 'more particles have energy greater than the activation energy'], 'D', 'Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution shifts.', 3],
        ['Powdered marble reacts faster with acid than marble chips because', ['it has a larger surface area', 'it is more concentrated', 'it is hotter', 'it has a catalyst'], 'A', 'More particles exposed for collisions.', 1],
        ['A catalyst increases the rate of a reaction by', ['raising the temperature', 'providing an alternative pathway with lower activation energy', 'increasing the concentration', 'being used up'], 'B', 'It is unchanged at the end.', 2],
        ['Increasing the pressure of a gaseous reaction increases the rate because', ['activation energy falls', 'temperature always falls', 'particles are closer together and collide more often', 'particles become heavier'], 'C', 'Like increasing concentration.', 2],
        ['The rate of a reaction can be measured by', ['the total mass of products only', 'the colour of the beaker', 'the volume of the flask', 'the change in concentration of a reactant or product per unit time'], 'D', 'For example cm³ of gas per second.', 2],
        ['Enzymes are', ['biological catalysts made of protein', 'inorganic acids', 'metals', 'reactants that are used up'], 'A', 'They are sensitive to temperature and pH.', 2],
        ['The catalyst used in the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide is often', ['iron', 'manganese(IV) oxide', 'platinum', 'nickel'], 'B', '2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂.', 2],
        ['On a graph of volume of gas against time, the reaction is fastest where', ['the curve is flat', 'the volume is largest', 'the gradient is steepest', 'time is greatest'], 'C', 'Usually at the start.', 2],
        ['The reaction rate decreases with time because', ['the catalyst runs out', 'the temperature always rises', 'more products are added', 'the concentration of reactants falls'], 'D', 'Fewer collisions per second.', 2],
        ['In the reaction of sodium thiosulphate with acid, the rate is followed by timing how long it takes for', ['a cross under the flask to disappear due to sulphur precipitate', 'a gas to be collected', 'the mass to rise', 'a colour change to blue'], 'A', 'The solution turns cloudy.', 3],
        ['Light speeds up reactions such as', ['neutralisation', 'the reaction of hydrogen with chlorine and photosynthesis', 'rusting in the dark', 'dissolving salt'], 'B', 'Photochemical reactions.', 3],
        ['30 cm³ of gas was collected in 60 s. Find the average rate of reaction.', ['2 cm³/s', '1,800 cm³/s', '0.5 cm³/s', '0.25 cm³/s'], 'C', 'Rate = volume ÷ time.', 2],
        ['30 cm³ of gas was collected in 40 s. Find the average rate of reaction.', ['1.33 cm³/s', '1,200 cm³/s', '0.38 cm³/s', '0.75 cm³/s'], 'D', 'Rate = volume ÷ time.', 2],
        ['48 cm³ of gas was collected in 60 s. Find the average rate of reaction.', ['0.8 cm³/s', '1.25 cm³/s', '2,880 cm³/s', '0.4 cm³/s'], 'A', 'Rate = volume ÷ time.', 2],
        ['The mass of a solid reactant fell from 2.4 g to 1.2 g in 10 s. Find the average rate of loss of reactant.', ['0.24 g/s', '0.12 g/s', '12 g/s', '1.12 g/s'], 'B', '(2.4 − 1.2) ÷ 10.', 3],
        ['40 cm³ of gas was collected in 40 s. Find the average rate of reaction.', ['1,600 cm³/s', '0.5 cm³/s', '1 cm³/s', '2 cm³/s'], 'C', 'Rate = volume ÷ time.', 2],
        ['The mass of a solid reactant fell from 2.4 g to 1.2 g in 20 s. Find the average rate of loss of reactant.', ['0.12 g/s', '24 g/s', '1.06 g/s', '0.06 g/s'], 'D', '(2.4 − 1.2) ÷ 20.', 3],
        ['The mass of a solid reactant fell from 2 g to 0.4 g in 40 s. Find the average rate of loss of reactant.', ['0.04 g/s', '0.01 g/s', '0.05 g/s', '64 g/s'], 'A', '(2 − 0.4) ÷ 40.', 3],
        ['The mass of a solid reactant fell from 2.4 g to 1.2 g in 40 s. Find the average rate of loss of reactant.', ['0.06 g/s', '0.03 g/s', '48 g/s', '1.03 g/s'], 'B', '(2.4 − 1.2) ÷ 40.', 3],
        ['A catalyst is chemically unchanged at the end of a reaction.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'It can be recovered.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['Raising the temperature by 10 °C roughly doubles the rate of many reactions.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'A useful rule of thumb.', 3, 'tf'],
        ['Increasing surface area increases the activation energy.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'It increases the collision frequency.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Enzymes work best at an optimum temperature.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Too high a temperature denatures them.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['All collisions between reactant particles lead to a reaction.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'Only effective collisions do.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Food is kept in a refrigerator to slow down the reactions that spoil it.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Lower temperature, slower rate.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['If 50 cm³ of hydrogen is produced in 25 s, the average rate is ___ cm³/s.', ['2'], '', '50 ÷ 25.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['If 45 cm³ of hydrogen is produced in 50 s, the average rate is ___ cm³/s.', ['0.9'], '', '45 ÷ 50.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['If 50 cm³ of hydrogen is produced in 50 s, the average rate is ___ cm³/s.', ['1'], '', '50 ÷ 50.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['If 45 cm³ of hydrogen is produced in 25 s, the average rate is ___ cm³/s.', ['1.8'], '', '45 ÷ 25.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['If 80 cm³ of hydrogen is produced in 20 s, the average rate is ___ cm³/s.', ['4'], '', '80 ÷ 20.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['If 30 cm³ of hydrogen is produced in 25 s, the average rate is ___ cm³/s.', ['1.2'], '', '30 ÷ 25.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Using collision theory, explain how each of the following affects the rate of reaction between calcium carbonate and hydrochloric acid: (a) concentration of acid, (b) temperature, (c) particle size of calcium carbonate.', ['Concentration: more frequent collisions', 'Temperature: more energetic collisions', 'Above activation energy', 'Surface area'], '(a) A higher concentration means more acid particles in a given volume, so collisions are more frequent and the rate increases. (b) A higher temperature gives particles more kinetic energy, so they collide more often and a greater fraction of collisions have energy equal to or greater than the activation energy. (c) Smaller particles (powder) have a larger total surface area, so more particles are exposed and collisions are more frequent.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Describe an experiment to show how the rate of reaction between magnesium ribbon and dilute hydrochloric acid changes with concentration. State the variables and sketch the expected graph.', ['Apparatus with gas syringe', 'Measurements over time', 'Variables', 'Graph shape', 'Steeper for higher concentration'], 'Add a fixed length of magnesium ribbon to a fixed volume of acid in a conical flask connected to a gas syringe. Record the volume of hydrogen every 10 seconds. Repeat with different concentrations of acid. Independent variable: acid concentration. Dependent variable: volume of gas (or time for the reaction). Controlled variables: length of Mg, volume of acid, temperature. The graph of volume against time rises steeply then levels off; higher concentrations give steeper initial gradients but the same final volume if Mg is limiting.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['With the aid of an energy profile diagram, explain how a catalyst increases the rate of a reaction. Give two industrial examples of catalysts.', ['Energy profile', 'Lower activation energy', 'More successful collisions', 'ΔH unchanged', 'Two examples'], 'The diagram shows reactants and products with a hump representing the activation energy; the catalysed pathway has a lower hump while ΔH is unchanged. A catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation energy, so more particles have enough energy to react when they collide and the rate increases. Examples: iron in the Haber process for ammonia; vanadium(V) oxide in the Contact process for sulphuric acid; nickel in the hydrogenation of oils; platinum in catalytic converters.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['Sketch the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution of molecular energies for a gas at two temperatures, T₁ and T₂ (T₂ > T₁). Use it to explain the effect of temperature on rate.', ['Curve shape', 'T₂ curve lower, shifted right', 'Same area', 'Ea marked', 'More molecules above Ea'], 'The curve starts at the origin, rises to a peak and tails off without touching the energy axis. At the higher temperature T₂ the peak is lower and shifted to higher energy, with the same area under the curve. The activation energy is marked on the energy axis; the area under the curve beyond Eₐ is much larger at T₂, showing that a greater proportion of molecules have energy at least equal to the activation energy, so the rate increases significantly.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain why (a) milk turns sour faster in hot weather, (b) wood shavings burn faster than a log, (c) a mixture of hydrogen and chlorine explodes in sunlight but reacts slowly in the dark.', ['Temperature and enzymes', 'Surface area', 'Light energy breaks bonds', 'Chain reaction'], '(a) Higher temperature makes the bacteria\'s enzyme-controlled reactions faster, so the milk sours sooner. (b) Wood shavings have a much larger surface area exposed to oxygen than a log, so collisions with oxygen are more frequent. (c) Light provides energy that breaks Cl–Cl bonds into reactive chlorine atoms (free radicals), starting a very fast chain reaction; in the dark few chlorine atoms form.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
    ],
    'SHS 2: Chemical equilibrium and Le Chatelier\'s principle' => [
        ['A reversible reaction is in dynamic equilibrium when', ['both reactions have stopped', 'only products are present', 'the rates of the forward and backward reactions are equal', 'the concentrations of reactants and products are always equal'], 'C', 'Concentrations stay constant.', 2],
        ['Le Chatelier\'s principle states that when a system at equilibrium is disturbed', ['the reaction stops', 'the equilibrium moves to increase the change', 'nothing happens', 'the equilibrium shifts to oppose the change'], 'D', 'It partly cancels the change.', 2],
        ['For N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ (ΔH negative), increasing the pressure', ['shifts equilibrium to the right', 'shifts equilibrium to the left', 'has no effect', 'stops the reaction'], 'A', 'Fewer gas moles on the right (2 vs 4).', 3],
        ['For N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ (ΔH negative), increasing the temperature', ['shifts equilibrium to the right', 'shifts equilibrium to the left', 'has no effect', 'increases the yield of ammonia'], 'B', 'It favours the endothermic backward reaction.', 3],
        ['The only factor that changes the value of Kc is', ['pressure', 'concentration', 'temperature', 'a catalyst'], 'C', 'Kc is constant at constant temperature.', 4],
        ['Removing ammonia as it forms in the Haber process', ['shifts equilibrium to the left', 'has no effect', 'stops the reaction', 'shifts equilibrium to the right, producing more ammonia'], 'D', 'The system replaces what is removed.', 3],
        ['The expression for Kc for 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃ is', ['[SO₃]² / ([SO₂]²[O₂])', '[SO₂]²[O₂] / [SO₃]²', '[SO₃] / ([SO₂][O₂])', '2[SO₃] / (2[SO₂][O₂])'], 'A', 'Products over reactants, powers = coefficients.', 4],
        ['A large value of Kc means', ['the equilibrium lies to the left', 'the equilibrium lies far to the right', 'the reaction is fast', 'the reaction is endothermic'], 'B', 'Mostly products.', 3],
        ['Adding acid to the yellow chromate equilibrium 2CrO₄²⁻ + 2H⁺ ⇌ Cr₂O₇²⁻ + H₂O turns the solution', ['yellow', 'green', 'orange', 'colourless'], 'C', 'Equilibrium shifts right to dichromate.', 4],
        ['The equilibrium N₂O₄ ⇌ 2NO₂ (ΔH positive) becomes darker brown when', ['cooled', 'compressed', 'a catalyst is added', 'heated'], 'D', 'NO₂ is brown; heating favours the endothermic forward reaction.', 4],
        ['For H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, the equilibrium concentrations are [H₂] = 0.4, [I₂] = 0.1 and [HI] = 0.8 mol/dm³. Find Kc.', ['16', '20', '0.063', '40'], 'A', 'Kc = [HI]² ÷ ([H₂][I₂]).', 4],
        ['For H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, the equilibrium concentrations are [H₂] = 0.4, [I₂] = 0.25 and [HI] = 0.4 mol/dm³. Find Kc.', ['4', '1.6', '0.625', '8'], 'B', 'Kc = [HI]² ÷ ([H₂][I₂]).', 4],
        ['For A ⇌ B, at equilibrium [A] = 0.5 mol/dm³ and [B] = 0.5 mol/dm³. Find Kc.', ['0.25', '2', '1', '0'], 'C', 'Kc = [B] ÷ [A].', 3],
        ['For H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, the equilibrium concentrations are [H₂] = 0.1, [I₂] = 0.25 and [HI] = 0.6 mol/dm³. Find Kc.', ['24', '0.069', '48', '14.4'], 'D', 'Kc = [HI]² ÷ ([H₂][I₂]).', 4],
        ['For H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, the equilibrium concentrations are [H₂] = 0.1, [I₂] = 0.2 and [HI] = 0.2 mol/dm³. Find Kc.', ['2', '10', '0.5', '20'], 'A', 'Kc = [HI]² ÷ ([H₂][I₂]).', 4],
        ['For H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, the equilibrium concentrations are [H₂] = 0.5, [I₂] = 0.5 and [HI] = 0.4 mol/dm³. Find Kc.', ['1.6', '0.64', '1.563', '3.2'], 'B', 'Kc = [HI]² ÷ ([H₂][I₂]).', 4],
        ['For A ⇌ B, at equilibrium [A] = 0.2 mol/dm³ and [B] = 0.4 mol/dm³. Find Kc.', ['0.5', '0.08', '2', '0.6'], 'C', 'Kc = [B] ÷ [A].', 3],
        ['For A ⇌ B, at equilibrium [A] = 0.2 mol/dm³ and [B] = 0.5 mol/dm³. Find Kc.', ['0.4', '0.1', '0.7', '2.5'], 'D', 'Kc = [B] ÷ [A].', 3],
        ['At equilibrium, the forward and backward reactions continue.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'It is dynamic.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Increasing the concentration of a reactant shifts equilibrium to the right.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'More product forms to use it up.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['A catalyst increases the yield at equilibrium.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'It only shortens the time to reach equilibrium.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['For an exothermic forward reaction, lowering the temperature increases Kc.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'The equilibrium shifts right.', 4, 'tf'],
        ['Equilibrium can only be reached in a closed system.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Nothing can escape.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Kc depends on pressure.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'Kc depends only on temperature.', 3, 'tf'],
        ['In the Haber process, the catalyst used is ___.', ['iron', 'finely divided iron'], '', 'With promoters such as K₂O.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['An equilibrium in which reactants and products are in the same phase is ___ equilibrium.', ['homogeneous'], '', 'For example all gases.', 4, 'fill'],
        ['The equilibrium constant expressed in terms of concentrations is given the symbol ___.', ['Kc', 'K_c'], '', 'Kp uses partial pressures.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['In N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ there are ___ moles of gas on the left-hand side.', ['4', 'four'], '', '1 + 3.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['In the Contact process, the catalyst used is vanadium(V) ___.', ['oxide', 'pentoxide'], '', 'Remember: V₂O₅.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['The French chemist whose principle predicts shifts in equilibrium was Henri Le ___.', ['Chatelier', 'Châtelier'], '', 'Le Chatelier\'s principle.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['The Haber process is N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), ΔH = −92 kJ/mol. (a) State and explain the effect on the yield of ammonia of increasing (i) pressure, (ii) temperature. (b) Explain why a temperature of about 450 °C and a pressure of about 200 atm are used. (c) State the role of iron.', ['Pressure shifts right', 'Temperature shifts left', 'Compromise temperature', 'Compromise pressure', 'Catalyst role'], '(a)(i) Increasing pressure shifts equilibrium to the right, the side with fewer gas molecules (2 instead of 4), so the yield increases. (ii) Increasing temperature shifts equilibrium to the left (the endothermic direction), so the yield decreases. (b) A lower temperature gives a better yield but the rate is too slow; 450 °C is a compromise between rate and yield. Very high pressures are expensive and dangerous, so about 200 atm is used as a compromise. (c) Iron is a catalyst: it speeds up the attainment of equilibrium without changing the yield.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['(a) Explain what is meant by dynamic equilibrium. (b) Write the Kc expression for 2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g). (c) At equilibrium, [NO₂] = 0.2 mol/dm³ and [N₂O₄] = 0.4 mol/dm³. Calculate Kc and state its units.', ['Equal rates', 'Constant concentrations', 'Kc expression', 'Kc = 10', 'Units mol⁻¹ dm³'], '(a) In a closed system, the forward and backward reactions take place at the same rate, so the concentrations of reactants and products stay constant though both reactions continue. (b) Kc = [N₂O₄] ÷ [NO₂]². (c) Kc = 0.4 ÷ 0.2² = 0.4 ÷ 0.04 = 10 mol⁻¹ dm³.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 7],
        ['Using Le Chatelier\'s principle, predict and explain the effect of each change on the equilibrium 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g), ΔH = −197 kJ/mol: (a) adding more oxygen, (b) increasing the temperature, (c) decreasing the pressure, (d) adding a catalyst.', ['More O₂: right', 'Higher T: left', 'Lower P: left', 'Catalyst: no shift'], '(a) Equilibrium shifts to the right to use up the extra oxygen, so more SO₃ forms. (b) Equilibrium shifts to the left, the endothermic direction, to absorb the added heat, so less SO₃ forms. (c) Equilibrium shifts to the left, the side with more gas molecules (3 vs 2), so less SO₃ forms. (d) No change in position; equilibrium is reached faster.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Describe a simple experiment that demonstrates the effect of temperature on an equilibrium, such as N₂O₄ ⇌ 2NO₂ or the cobalt chloride equilibrium. Explain your observations.', ['Two sealed tubes, hot and cold', 'Colour observations', 'Forward reaction endothermic', 'Shift explained'], 'Seal brown NO₂/N₂O₄ gas in two tubes. Place one in ice water and one in hot water. The cold tube becomes paler and the hot one darker brown. The forward reaction N₂O₄ → 2NO₂ is endothermic: heating shifts the equilibrium to the right, producing more brown NO₂; cooling shifts it to the left, forming colourless N₂O₄. (Alternatively, pink [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ turns blue [CoCl₄]²⁻ on heating, because the forward reaction is endothermic.)', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain why, in industry, a catalyst is used in reversible reactions even though it does not change the position of equilibrium.', ['Lowers activation energy both ways', 'Faster attainment of equilibrium', 'Lower temperature possible', 'Economic benefit'], 'A catalyst lowers the activation energy of both forward and backward reactions equally, so equilibrium is reached much more quickly. This allows a reasonable rate at a lower temperature, which can give a better yield for exothermic reactions, saves energy costs and increases the amount of product made per day.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 5],
    ],
    'SHS 2: Acid-base theories and strength' => [
        ['According to Arrhenius, an acid is a substance that', ['produces hydrogen ions (H⁺) in water', 'accepts protons', 'donates electron pairs', 'produces OH⁻ ions in water'], 'A', 'For example HCl.', 2],
        ['According to Brønsted–Lowry theory, an acid is a', ['proton acceptor', 'proton donor', 'electron pair acceptor', 'hydroxide donor'], 'B', 'It donates H⁺.', 2],
        ['According to Brønsted–Lowry theory, a base is a', ['proton donor', 'electron pair acceptor', 'proton acceptor', 'hydrogen ion producer'], 'C', 'It accepts H⁺.', 2],
        ['A Lewis acid is', ['an electron pair donor', 'a proton donor only', 'a hydroxide ion', 'an electron pair acceptor'], 'D', 'For example BF₃.', 4],
        ['A Lewis base is', ['an electron pair donor', 'an electron pair acceptor', 'a proton donor', 'always a metal'], 'A', 'For example NH₃.', 4],
        ['The conjugate base of HCl is', ['H⁺', 'Cl⁻', 'H₃O⁺', 'OH⁻'], 'B', 'HCl loses a proton.', 3],
        ['The conjugate acid of NH₃ is', ['NH₂⁻', 'H⁺', 'NH₄⁺', 'N₂'], 'C', 'NH₃ gains a proton.', 3],
        ['An alkali is', ['any acid', 'an insoluble base', 'a salt', 'a base that is soluble in water'], 'D', 'For example NaOH.', 1],
        ['An amphoteric oxide reacts with', ['both acids and bases', 'acids only', 'bases only', 'neither acids nor bases'], 'A', 'For example ZnO and Al₂O₃.', 3],
        ['The basicity of an acid is', ['its pH', 'the number of replaceable hydrogen ions per molecule', 'its concentration', 'its molar mass'], 'B', 'H₂SO₄ is dibasic.', 3],
        ['Which acid is tribasic?', ['HCl', 'H₂SO₄', 'H₃PO₄', 'CH₃COOH'], 'C', 'Three replaceable H atoms.', 3],
        ['In the reaction H₂O + NH₃ ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻, water acts as', ['a base', 'a salt', 'a catalyst', 'an acid'], 'D', 'It donates a proton.', 4],
        ['How many moles of NaOH are needed to neutralise 0.2 mol of carbonic acid completely?', ['0.4 mol', '0.2 mol', '0.1 mol', '0.6 mol'], 'A', 'carbonic acid is dibasic.', 4],
        ['What is the basicity of phosphoric acid, H₃PO₄?', ['4', '3', '2', '5'], 'B', 'Count the replaceable hydrogens.', 3],
        ['What is the basicity of sulphuric acid, H₂SO₄?', ['3', '1', '2', '4'], 'C', 'Count the replaceable hydrogens.', 3],
        ['How many moles of NaOH are needed to neutralise 0.5 mol of sulphuric acid completely?', ['0.5 mol', '0.25 mol', '1.5 mol', '1 mol'], 'D', 'sulphuric acid is dibasic.', 4],
