        ['A base that dissolves in water is called an ___.', ['alkali'], '', 'For example KOH.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['In Lewis theory, a base is an electron ___ donor.', ['pair'], '', 'For example NH₃.', 4, 'fill'],
        ['Compare the Arrhenius, Brønsted–Lowry and Lewis definitions of acids and bases, giving an example for each.', ['Arrhenius with example', 'Brønsted–Lowry with example', 'Lewis with example', 'Breadth comparison'], 'Arrhenius: an acid produces H⁺ ions in water (HCl → H⁺ + Cl⁻); a base produces OH⁻ ions in water (NaOH). Brønsted–Lowry: an acid is a proton donor and a base is a proton acceptor; in NH₃ + HCl → NH₄⁺ + Cl⁻, HCl is the acid and NH₃ the base. Lewis: an acid is an electron pair acceptor and a base an electron pair donor; in BF₃ + NH₃ → F₃B←NH₃, BF₃ is the acid and NH₃ the base. Each theory is broader than the one before.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Distinguish between a strong acid and a concentrated acid. Explain, with equations, why 0.1 mol/dm³ hydrochloric acid has a lower pH than 0.1 mol/dm³ ethanoic acid.', ['Strength vs concentration', 'HCl full ionisation', 'Ethanoic partial ionisation', '[H⁺] comparison'], 'Strength is the degree of ionisation; concentration is the amount of acid dissolved per unit volume. A strong acid ionises completely; a concentrated acid has a lot of acid per dm³. HCl → H⁺ + Cl⁻ ionises completely, so [H⁺] = 0.1 mol/dm³ and pH = 1. CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺ ionises only slightly, so [H⁺] is much less than 0.1 mol/dm³ and the pH is about 3.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['Identify the conjugate acid–base pairs in each reaction: (a) HNO₃ + H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺ + NO₃⁻, (b) NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻, (c) HCO₃⁻ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ + OH⁻.', ['Pairs in (a)', 'Pairs in (b)', 'Pairs in (c)', 'Water amphiprotic'], '(a) HNO₃ / NO₃⁻ and H₃O⁺ / H₂O. (b) NH₄⁺ / NH₃ and H₂O / OH⁻. (c) H₂CO₃ / HCO₃⁻ and H₂O / OH⁻. In (a) water acts as a base; in (b) and (c) water acts as an acid, showing that water is amphiprotic.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['State four properties of acids and write a balanced equation for each property using dilute hydrochloric acid.', ['Metal property and equation', 'Base property', 'Carbonate property', 'Alkali or indicator property'], '1. Acids react with reactive metals to give a salt and hydrogen: Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂. 2. Acids react with bases to give a salt and water: CuO + 2HCl → CuCl₂ + H₂O. 3. Acids react with carbonates to give a salt, water and carbon dioxide: CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂. 4. Acids neutralise alkalis: NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O. Also: acids turn blue litmus red and have a sour taste.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain, with equations, what is meant by an amphoteric oxide, using zinc oxide as an example.', ['Definition', 'Reaction with acid', 'Reaction with alkali', 'Zincate', 'Other example'], 'An amphoteric oxide reacts with both acids and bases to form salts. Zinc oxide reacts with hydrochloric acid as a base: ZnO + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂O. It reacts with sodium hydroxide as an acid: ZnO + 2NaOH + H₂O → Na₂Zn(OH)₄ (sodium zincate). Aluminium oxide and lead(II) oxide behave similarly.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 5],
    ],
