        ['The flame colour of copper(II) ions is ___.', ['blue-green', 'green', 'bluish-green', 'blue green'], '', 'A characteristic flame test.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Describe how you would prepare a pure dry sample of copper(II) sulphate crystals from copper(II) oxide and dilute sulphuric acid. Write the equation.', ['Excess CuO added to warm acid', 'Filter excess', 'Concentrate filtrate', 'Crystallise and dry', 'Equation'], 'Warm dilute sulphuric acid in a beaker. Add copper(II) oxide a little at a time with stirring until some remains undissolved (excess), so all the acid is used. Filter off the excess oxide. Heat the blue filtrate to evaporate some water until it is saturated (crystals form on a glass rod). Allow it to cool so that crystals form. Filter off the crystals, wash with a little cold water and dry between filter papers. CuO + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Describe how to prepare a sample of lead(II) iodide by precipitation. Write the ionic equation.', ['Two soluble salts', 'Mix to precipitate', 'Filter and wash', 'Dry', 'Ionic equation'], 'Dissolve lead(II) nitrate in distilled water and potassium iodide in distilled water in separate beakers. Mix the two solutions; a bright yellow precipitate of lead(II) iodide forms. Filter the mixture, wash the residue with distilled water to remove soluble potassium nitrate and dry it in a warm oven. Ionic equation: Pb²⁺(aq) + 2I⁻(aq) → PbI₂(s).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['A solution X gives the following results. (i) With NaOH(aq), a white precipitate forms which dissolves in excess. (ii) With NH₃(aq), a white precipitate forms which dissolves in excess. (iii) With acidified BaCl₂(aq), a white precipitate forms. Identify the cation and anion in X and explain each result.', ['Amphoteric hydroxide', 'Zinc from NH₃ test', 'Sulphate from BaCl₂', 'ZnSO₄'], '(i) A white precipitate soluble in excess NaOH suggests Zn²⁺, Al³⁺ or Pb²⁺. (ii) A white precipitate soluble in excess NH₃ is characteristic of Zn²⁺ (aluminium and lead hydroxides do not dissolve in excess ammonia). So the cation is Zn²⁺. (iii) A white precipitate with acidified BaCl₂ shows sulphate ions, forming insoluble BaSO₄. X is zinc sulphate, ZnSO₄.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['State the tests and observations for the gases (a) hydrogen, (b) oxygen, (c) carbon dioxide, (d) ammonia, (e) chlorine.', ['H₂ pop', 'O₂ glowing splint', 'CO₂ lime water', 'NH₃ red litmus blue', 'Cl₂ bleaches'], '(a) Hydrogen: a lighted splint gives a squeaky pop. (b) Oxygen: relights a glowing splint. (c) Carbon dioxide: turns lime water milky. (d) Ammonia: turns damp red litmus paper blue; forms white fumes with HCl. (e) Chlorine: bleaches damp litmus paper (blue litmus turns red then white).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 2, 'theory', 5],
        ['Explain, with examples, the differences between normal salts, acid salts, basic salts and double salts.', ['Normal and example', 'Acid salt and example', 'Basic salt and example', 'Double salt and example'], 'A normal salt has all the replaceable hydrogen of the acid replaced by a metal or ammonium ion, for example NaCl and Na₂SO₄. An acid salt has only part of the replaceable hydrogen replaced, for example NaHSO₄ and NaHCO₃. A basic salt contains hydroxide or oxide ions as well as the anion of the acid, for example Mg(OH)Cl and basic lead carbonate. A double salt contains two different cations (or anions) crystallised together, for example potash alum, KAl(SO₄)₂·12H₂O.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
    ],
