        ['Describe the reactions of lithium, sodium and potassium with cold water. Write a general equation and explain the trend in reactivity.', ['Observations Li', 'Observations Na', 'Observations K', 'General equation', 'Explanation of trend'], 'All three float and react to give the hydroxide and hydrogen; the solution becomes alkaline. Lithium reacts steadily with fizzing. Sodium melts into a ball and darts about the surface. Potassium reacts violently and the hydrogen ignites with a lilac flame. General equation: 2M + 2H₂O → 2MOH + H₂. Reactivity increases down the group because atoms get larger, the outer electron is further from the nucleus and more shielded, so it is lost more easily.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Describe the lime cycle: the conversion of limestone to quicklime, slaked lime and back to calcium carbonate. Give an equation for each step and one use of each product.', ['Decomposition equation', 'Slaking equation', 'Carbonation equation', 'Uses'], 'Heating limestone in a lime kiln: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂; quicklime is used to make cement and to dry gases. Adding water to quicklime: CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂; slaked lime is used to neutralise acidic soils and in mortar. Passing carbon dioxide into lime water: Ca(OH)₂ + CO₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O; calcium carbonate is used in building and making glass.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain why sodium is more reactive than magnesium in terms of their electron configurations and ionisation energies.', ['Configurations', 'One vs two electrons', 'Nuclear charge', 'Ionisation energy comparison'], 'Sodium (2, 8, 1) needs to lose only one electron to form Na⁺, while magnesium (2, 8, 2) must lose two electrons to form Mg²⁺. Magnesium has a greater nuclear charge in the same shell, so its outer electrons are held more strongly. The total energy needed to remove two electrons from Mg is much greater than to remove one from Na, so sodium reacts more readily.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 5],
        ['Describe the manufacture of sodium hydroxide by the electrolysis of brine. State the products at each electrode and two uses of sodium hydroxide.', ['Brine electrolysis', 'Anode chlorine', 'Cathode hydrogen', 'NaOH left', 'Two uses'], 'Concentrated sodium chloride solution (brine) is electrolysed in a membrane (or diaphragm) cell. At the anode, chloride ions are oxidised to chlorine gas: 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻. At the cathode, water is reduced to hydrogen: 2H₂O + 2e⁻ → H₂ + 2OH⁻. Na⁺ and OH⁻ remain, giving sodium hydroxide solution. Uses: making soap, paper, detergents, and in textile and aluminium processing.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['Compare group 1 and group 2 elements in terms of (a) charge on the ion, (b) reactivity with water, (c) solubility of carbonates, (d) thermal stability of carbonates.', ['Ion charges', 'Reactivity', 'Carbonate solubility', 'Thermal stability'], '(a) Group 1 forms M⁺ ions; group 2 forms M²⁺ ions. (b) Group 1 metals react vigorously with cold water; group 2 metals react more slowly (Mg very slowly with cold water, quickly with steam). (c) Group 1 carbonates are soluble (except Li₂CO₃ sparingly); group 2 carbonates are insoluble. (d) Group 1 carbonates are thermally stable (except Li₂CO₃); group 2 carbonates decompose on heating to give the oxide and CO₂.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
    ],
