        ['When chlorine reacts with potassium iodide solution, the solution turns brown because ___ is formed.', ['iodine', 'I₂', 'I2'], '', 'Cl₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + I₂.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['The most reactive halogen is ___.', ['fluorine', 'F₂', 'F2'], '', 'At the top of group 17.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['Chlorine reacts with water to form hydrochloric acid and ___ acid.', ['chloric(I)', 'hypochlorous', 'chloric(i)', 'HOCl'], '', 'Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HCl + HOCl.', 4, 'fill'],
        ['Describe the laboratory preparation of dry chlorine gas. Include the reagents, the method of drying and the method of collection, with an equation.', ['Reagents', 'Equation', 'Remove HCl with water', 'Dry with conc. H₂SO₄', 'Downward delivery in fume cupboard'], 'Concentrated hydrochloric acid is dripped onto manganese(IV) oxide and warmed: MnO₂ + 4HCl → MnCl₂ + Cl₂ + 2H₂O (or KMnO₄ with concentrated HCl in the cold). The gas is passed through water to remove HCl fumes, then through concentrated sulphuric acid to dry it. It is collected by downward delivery (upward displacement of air) because it is denser than air, in a fume cupboard since it is poisonous.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Describe and explain what happens when chlorine water is added separately to solutions of potassium bromide and potassium iodide. Write ionic equations and explain what this shows about reactivity.', ['KBr observation', 'Ionic equation for Br', 'KI observation', 'Ionic equation for I', 'Reactivity order'], 'With potassium bromide, the colourless solution turns orange-yellow because bromine is displaced: Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂. With potassium iodide, it turns brown (black solid may form) because iodine is displaced: Cl₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + I₂. Chlorine is a stronger oxidising agent than bromine and iodine; it gains electrons more readily, so reactivity decreases down the group.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Give three uses each of (a) chlorine and (b) the noble gases, and explain one property that makes each use possible.', ['Three chlorine uses', 'Property for chlorine', 'Three noble gas uses', 'Property for noble gas'], '(a) Chlorine: sterilising drinking water and swimming pools (kills bacteria); making bleach (oxidising agent); making PVC plastics and solvents. (b) Noble gases: argon in light bulbs (inert); helium in balloons (light, non-flammable); neon in advertising signs (glows when electricity passes); argon as a shield gas in welding (unreactive).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain the trends down group 17 in (a) physical state and colour, (b) reactivity, (c) boiling point.', ['State and colour trend', 'Reactivity decreases', 'Reason: atomic size and shielding', 'Boiling point increases with van der Waals'], '(a) Fluorine is a pale yellow gas, chlorine a greenish-yellow gas, bromine a red-brown liquid and iodine a grey-black solid: the colour darkens and the state changes from gas to solid. (b) Reactivity decreases down the group because the atoms get larger and the incoming electron is further from the nucleus and more shielded, so it is attracted less strongly. (c) Boiling point increases because the molecules have more electrons, so the van der Waals forces between them are stronger.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain why the noble gases were once called inert gases, and why that name is no longer fully accurate.', ['Full outer shells', 'No compounds known', 'Xenon compounds made', 'Lower ionisation energy of heavy members'], 'The noble gases have full outer shells (2 electrons for helium, 8 for the others), so they have very little tendency to lose, gain or share electrons. For a long time no compounds were known and they were called inert. In 1962 xenon was made to react with platinum hexafluoride, and later compounds such as XeF₂, XeF₄ and KrF₂ were made. The heavier noble gases have lower ionisation energies, so they can react with very electronegative elements like fluorine.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 5],
    ],
    'SHS 2: Hydrocarbons: alkanes, alkenes and alkynes' => [
        ['Alkanes are described as saturated because', ['they dissolve in water', 'they contain double bonds', 'they are wet', 'they contain only single carbon–carbon bonds'], 'D', 'Each carbon has four single bonds.', 1],
        ['The main reaction of alkanes with chlorine in sunlight is', ['substitution', 'addition', 'polymerisation', 'neutralisation'], 'A', 'H atoms are replaced by Cl.', 2],
        ['The characteristic reaction of alkenes is', ['substitution', 'addition', 'neutralisation', 'esterification'], 'B', 'The C=C bond opens.', 2],
        ['Ethene reacts with hydrogen in the presence of nickel to form', ['ethanol', 'ethyne', 'ethane', 'polyethene'], 'C', 'Hydrogenation.', 2],
        ['Ethene reacts with steam (phosphoric acid catalyst) to form', ['ethane', 'ethanoic acid', 'ethyne', 'ethanol'], 'D', 'Hydration.', 3],
        ['Cracking is the process of', ['breaking large hydrocarbon molecules into smaller ones', 'joining small molecules', 'burning hydrocarbons', 'dissolving hydrocarbons'], 'A', 'Gives petrol and alkenes.', 2],
        ['The complete combustion of methane produces', ['carbon monoxide only', 'carbon dioxide and water', 'carbon and hydrogen', 'methanol'], 'B', 'CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O.', 1],
        ['Incomplete combustion of hydrocarbons produces', ['only carbon dioxide', 'oxygen', 'carbon monoxide and soot', 'hydrogen'], 'C', 'Limited oxygen supply.', 2],
        ['Carbon monoxide is poisonous because it', ['is acidic', 'is coloured', 'causes acid rain', 'combines with haemoglobin and stops it carrying oxygen'], 'D', 'Carboxyhaemoglobin forms.', 2],
        ['Ethyne is prepared in the laboratory by adding water to', ['calcium carbide', 'calcium carbonate', 'ethanol', 'sodium'], 'A', 'CaC₂ + 2H₂O → C₂H₂ + Ca(OH)₂.', 3],
        ['Ethyne is used in oxy-acetylene flames for', ['making soap', 'welding and cutting metals', 'bleaching', 'cooling'], 'B', 'Very high temperature.', 2],
