        ['The fraction of crude oil with the lowest boiling point is', ['bitumen', 'diesel', 'kerosene', 'refinery gas'], 'D', 'It leaves at the top.', 2],
        ['The product of the addition of HBr to ethene is', ['bromoethane', '1,2-dibromoethane', 'ethanol', 'ethane'], 'A', 'CH₂=CH₂ + HBr → CH₃CH₂Br.', 3],
        ['The product of bromine adding to ethene is', ['bromoethane', '1,2-dibromoethane', 'bromoethene', 'ethane'], 'B', 'Br adds to both carbons.', 3],
        ['Acidified potassium manganate(VII) turns from purple to colourless with', ['alkanes', 'noble gases', 'alkenes', 'carbon dioxide'], 'C', 'Oxidation of C=C.', 3],
        ['Benzene, C₆H₆, is an example of an', ['alkane', 'alkyne', 'alkanol', 'aromatic hydrocarbon'], 'D', 'It has a delocalised ring of electrons.', 4],
        ['LPG (liquefied petroleum gas) used for cooking is mainly', ['propane and butane', 'methane only', 'ethene', 'octane'], 'A', 'Stored under pressure as a liquid.', 2],
        ['Alkenes undergo addition reactions.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'The double bond opens.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['Methane decolourises bromine water quickly in the dark.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'Alkanes do not react with bromine water.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Cracking produces alkenes.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'For example ethene.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Ethyne contains a triple bond.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'H–C≡C–H.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['Hydrogenation of vegetable oils produces margarine.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Addition of H₂ with a nickel catalyst.', 3, 'tf'],
        ['Bitumen has a lower boiling point than petrol.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'Bitumen has a very high boiling point.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['The catalyst used for hydrogenating alkenes is ___.', ['nickel', 'Ni', 'finely divided nickel'], '', 'At about 150 °C.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['The test for an alkene is that it decolourises ___ water.', ['bromine'], '', 'Orange to colourless.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['The type of reaction between methane and chlorine in sunlight is ___.', ['substitution', 'free radical substitution', 'free-radical substitution'], '', 'H replaced by Cl.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Breaking down large alkane molecules into smaller ones is called ___.', ['cracking'], '', 'Thermal or catalytic.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Ethyne is prepared by adding water to calcium ___.', ['carbide', 'dicarbide'], '', 'Remember: CaC₂.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['The gas produced by incomplete combustion that is poisonous is carbon ___.', ['monoxide'], '', 'Remember: CO.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['Describe a chemical test to distinguish between ethane and ethene. Write an equation for the reaction that takes place.', ['Bromine water', 'Ethene decolourises', 'Ethane no change', 'Equation'], 'Bubble each gas through bromine water (orange). Ethene decolourises the bromine water quickly because bromine adds across the double bond; ethane has no effect in the dark. CH₂=CH₂ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br (1,2-dibromoethane). Alternatively, ethene decolourises acidified potassium manganate(VII).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 2, 'theory', 5],
        ['Explain the fractional distillation of crude oil. Name four fractions and give one use of each.', ['Vaporise crude oil', 'Temperature gradient', 'Condense by boiling point', 'Four fractions', 'Uses'], 'Crude oil is heated to vaporise it and passed into a fractionating column that is hot at the bottom and cooler at the top. The vapours rise; each fraction condenses at a height where the temperature is just below its boiling point. Small molecules with low boiling points leave near the top; large molecules leave near the bottom. Fractions: refinery gas (cooking gas, LPG); petrol (fuel for cars); kerosene (jet fuel, lamps); diesel (fuel for lorries and generators); lubricating oil (lubrication); bitumen (road surfacing).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['(a) What is cracking? (b) Write an equation for the cracking of decane, C₁₀H₂₂, to give octane and ethene. (c) State two reasons why cracking is important.', ['Definition', 'Catalyst or heat', 'Balanced equation', 'Petrol demand', 'Alkenes for plastics'], '(a) Cracking is the breaking down of large, less useful hydrocarbon molecules into smaller, more useful ones using heat (thermal) or a catalyst such as aluminium oxide or zeolite. (b) C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄. (c) It produces more petrol, which is in high demand, from heavier fractions; it produces alkenes such as ethene for making plastics and ethanol.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Write equations for the reactions of ethene with (a) hydrogen, (b) bromine, (c) hydrogen chloride, (d) steam. Name the products and state the conditions where needed.', ['Hydrogenation', 'Bromination', 'HCl addition', 'Hydration with conditions'], '(a) CH₂=CH₂ + H₂ → CH₃CH₃, ethane; nickel catalyst at about 150 °C. (b) CH₂=CH₂ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br, 1,2-dibromoethane; room temperature. (c) CH₂=CH₂ + HCl → CH₃CH₂Cl, chloroethane. (d) CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O → CH₃CH₂OH, ethanol; phosphoric acid catalyst, about 300 °C and 60 atm.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Describe the laboratory preparation of ethyne and give two of its properties and two uses.', ['Calcium carbide and water', 'Equation', 'Collection over water', 'Two properties', 'Two uses'], 'Water is dripped slowly from a dropping funnel onto calcium carbide in a flask: CaC₂ + 2H₂O → C₂H₂ + Ca(OH)₂. The gas is collected over water. Properties: colourless gas; burns with a very sooty, luminous flame in air; decolourises bromine water (addition reaction); slightly soluble in water. Uses: oxy-acetylene welding and cutting; making plastics such as PVC; ripening fruit artificially.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
    ],
    'SHS 2: Alkanols and alkanoic acids' => [
        ['The functional group in ethanol is', ['carboxyl (–COOH)', 'hydroxyl (–OH)', 'carbonyl (C=O)', 'amino (–NH₂)'], 'B', 'Alkanol.', 1],
        ['In fermentation, yeast provides the enzyme', ['amylase', 'pepsin', 'zymase', 'lipase'], 'C', 'C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂.', 3],
        ['Fermentation must be carried out in the absence of air because', ['yeast needs oxygen to produce ethanol', 'air contains too much nitrogen', 'it would explode', 'oxygen would oxidise ethanol to ethanoic acid'], 'D', 'Anaerobic conditions.', 3],
        ['Primary alkanols are oxidised by acidified potassium dichromate(VI) first to', ['alkanals, then alkanoic acids', 'alkenes', 'alkanes', 'esters'], 'A', 'Orange dichromate turns green.', 4],
        ['A secondary alkanol is oxidised to', ['an alkanoic acid', 'an alkanone (ketone)', 'an alkane', 'an ester'], 'B', 'For example propan-2-ol → propanone.', 4],
        ['Tertiary alkanols are', ['oxidised to acids', 'oxidised to ketones', 'not easily oxidised by acidified dichromate', 'reduced to alkanes'], 'C', 'No H on the carbon bearing –OH.', 5],
        ['Ethanol reacts with sodium to produce', ['sodium hydroxide', 'ethene', 'ethanoic acid', 'sodium ethoxide and hydrogen'], 'D', '2C₂H₅OH + 2Na → 2C₂H₅ONa + H₂.', 3],
        ['Dehydration of ethanol with concentrated sulphuric acid gives', ['ethene', 'ethane', 'ethanoic acid', 'ethyl ethanoate'], 'A', 'Loss of water at 170 °C.', 3],
        ['Ethanoic acid is the main acid in', ['lemon juice', 'vinegar', 'sour milk', 'car batteries'], 'B', 'About 5%.', 1],
        ['Ethanoic acid reacts with sodium carbonate to give', ['ethanol and CO₂', 'hydrogen and sodium', 'sodium ethanoate, carbon dioxide and water', 'ethyl ethanoate'], 'C', 'Typical acid reaction.', 2],
        ['An alkanoic acid reacts with an alkanol in the presence of concentrated H₂SO₄ to form', ['a salt and hydrogen', 'an alkene', 'a soap', 'an ester and water'], 'D', 'Esterification.', 2],
