        ['The ester CH₃COOCH₃ is named methyl ___.', ['ethanoate'], '', 'From methanol and ethanoic acid.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['Propan-2-ol is oxidised to the ketone ___.', ['propanone', 'acetone'], '', 'A secondary alkanol gives a ketone.', 4, 'fill'],
        ['Describe the production of ethanol by fermentation. Write the equation, state the conditions and explain how the ethanol is separated from the mixture.', ['Sugar and yeast', 'Temperature 25–35 °C', 'Anaerobic', 'Equation', 'Fractional distillation'], 'A sugar solution (glucose or molasses) is mixed with yeast and kept at about 25–35 °C in the absence of air. Zymase in yeast catalyses C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂. Air is excluded so that ethanol is not oxidised to ethanoic acid. Fermentation stops at about 12–15% ethanol because the yeast is killed. The mixture is filtered and then fractionally distilled to separate ethanol (boiling point 78 °C) from water.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Write equations and name the products for the reactions of ethanol with (a) sodium, (b) excess oxygen (combustion), (c) acidified potassium dichromate(VI), (d) ethanoic acid.', ['With sodium', 'Combustion', 'Oxidation with colour change', 'Esterification'], '(a) 2C₂H₅OH + 2Na → 2C₂H₅ONa + H₂: sodium ethoxide and hydrogen. (b) C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O: carbon dioxide and water. (c) C₂H₅OH + 2[O] → CH₃COOH + H₂O: ethanoic acid (via ethanal); the orange dichromate turns green. (d) CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O: ethyl ethanoate (concentrated H₂SO₄ catalyst).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Classify the following alkanols as primary, secondary or tertiary and explain your choice: ethanol, propan-2-ol, 2-methylpropan-2-ol. Describe how acidified potassium dichromate can distinguish them.', ['Ethanol primary', 'Propan-2-ol secondary', 'Tertiary example', 'Dichromate colour change', 'Tertiary no change'], 'Ethanol, CH₃CH₂OH, is primary: the carbon bearing –OH is attached to one other carbon. Propan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₃, is secondary: that carbon is attached to two carbons. 2-methylpropan-2-ol, (CH₃)₃COH, is tertiary: that carbon is attached to three carbons. On warming with acidified K₂Cr₂O₇, primary and secondary alkanols turn the orange solution green (primary gives an aldehyde then acid; secondary gives a ketone), but the tertiary alkanol does not change the colour.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Describe how you would prepare a sample of ethyl ethanoate in the laboratory. State one use of esters.', ['Reagents and catalyst', 'Heat under reflux', 'Neutralise excess acid', 'Fruity oily layer', 'Use'], 'Mix ethanol and glacial ethanoic acid in a flask and add a few drops of concentrated sulphuric acid as a catalyst. Heat under reflux for some time. Pour the mixture into sodium carbonate solution to remove excess acid; the ester forms an oily layer with a fruity smell, which can be separated and distilled. CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O. Uses: flavourings, perfumes, solvents for glues and nail varnish.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Compare ethanol and ethanoic acid in terms of (a) functional group, (b) effect on litmus, (c) reaction with sodium carbonate, (d) one use each.', ['Functional groups', 'Litmus', 'Sodium carbonate test', 'Uses'], '(a) Ethanol has a hydroxyl group (–OH); ethanoic acid has a carboxyl group (–COOH). (b) Ethanol is neutral and has no effect on litmus; ethanoic acid turns blue litmus red. (c) Ethanol does not react with sodium carbonate; ethanoic acid reacts with effervescence, giving CO₂: 2CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2CH₃COONa + CO₂ + H₂O. (d) Ethanol is used as a fuel or solvent; ethanoic acid is used as vinegar for preserving food.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
    ],
