    'SHS 3: Redox: oxidation numbers and balancing' => [
        ['Reduction is', ['gain of electrons or decrease in oxidation number', 'loss of electrons', 'gain of oxygen', 'increase in oxidation number'], 'A', 'OIL RIG.', 1],
        ['An oxidising agent', ['loses electrons', 'accepts electrons and is itself reduced', 'is itself oxidised', 'always contains oxygen'], 'B', 'For example KMnO₄.', 2],
        ['The oxidation number of an uncombined element is', ['+1', '−1', '0', 'equal to its group number'], 'C', 'For example O₂ or Fe.', 1],
        ['In Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu, the species oxidised is', ['Cu²⁺', 'Zn²⁺', 'Cu', 'Zn'], 'D', 'Zn loses electrons.', 2],
        ['In 2Fe²⁺ + Cl₂ → 2Fe³⁺ + 2Cl⁻, the oxidising agent is', ['Cl₂', 'Fe²⁺', 'Fe³⁺', 'Cl⁻'], 'A', 'Chlorine gains electrons.', 3],
        ['A disproportionation reaction is one in which', ['no electrons are transferred', 'the same element is both oxidised and reduced', 'two different metals are oxidised', 'only oxygen is involved'], 'B', 'For example Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaOCl + H₂O.', 5],
        ['Acidified potassium manganate(VII) changes colour from', ['colourless to purple', 'orange to green', 'purple to colourless when reduced', 'blue to red'], 'C', 'MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺.', 3],
        ['Acidified potassium dichromate(VI) changes colour from', ['green to orange', 'purple to colourless', 'yellow to blue', 'orange to green when reduced'], 'D', 'Cr₂O₇²⁻ → Cr³⁺.', 3],
        ['What is the oxidation number of S in SO₂?', ['+4', '−4', '+2', '+5'], 'A', 'Use O = −2, H = +1 and the overall charge.', 3],
        ['What is the oxidation number of Cl in ClO₃⁻?', ['−5', '+5', '+3', '+6'], 'B', 'Use O = −2, H = +1 and the overall charge.', 3],
        ['What is the oxidation number of N in NH₄⁺?', ['+3', '−5', '−3', '−2'], 'C', 'Use O = −2, H = +1 and the overall charge.', 3],
        ['What is the oxidation number of Fe in Fe₂O₃?', ['−3', '+1', '+4', '+3'], 'D', 'Use O = −2, H = +1 and the overall charge.', 3],
        ['What is the oxidation number of C in CO₂?', ['+4', '−4', '+2', '+5'], 'A', 'Use O = −2, H = +1 and the overall charge.', 3],
        ['What is the oxidation number of S in Na₂S₂O₃?', ['−2', '+2', '0', '+3'], 'B', 'Use O = −2, H = +1 and the overall charge.', 3],
        ['What is the oxidation number of Cr in CrO₄²⁻?', ['−6', '+4', '+6', '+7'], 'C', 'Use O = −2, H = +1 and the overall charge.', 3],
        ['What is the oxidation number of N in NO₃⁻?', ['−5', '+3', '+6', '+5'], 'D', 'Use O = −2, H = +1 and the overall charge.', 3],
        ['What is the oxidation number of S in SO₄²⁻?', ['+6', '−6', '+4', '+7'], 'A', 'Use O = −2, H = +1 and the overall charge.', 3],
        ['What is the oxidation number of C in CO?', ['−2', '+2', '0', '+3'], 'B', 'Use O = −2, H = +1 and the overall charge.', 3],
        ['Oxidation and reduction always take place together.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Electrons lost by one species are gained by another.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['The oxidation number of hydrogen in metal hydrides such as NaH is +1.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'In metal hydrides it is −1.', 4, 'tf'],
        ['The rusting of iron is a redox reaction.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Iron is oxidised by oxygen.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['In Fe³⁺ + e⁻ → Fe²⁺, iron is reduced.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'It gains an electron.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Hydrogen peroxide can act as both an oxidising and a reducing agent.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Oxygen is in the −1 state.', 5, 'tf'],
        ['The sum of oxidation numbers in a neutral compound is always 1.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'It is zero.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['The oxidation number of Cl in ClO₃⁻ is ___.', ['+5', '5'], '', 'Sum of oxidation numbers = overall charge.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['The oxidation number of Cl in ClO⁻ is ___.', ['+1', '1'], '', 'Sum of oxidation numbers = overall charge.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['The oxidation number of Cr in K₂Cr₂O₇ is ___.', ['+6', '6'], '', 'Sum of oxidation numbers = overall charge.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['The oxidation number of N in NH₄⁺ is ___.', ['−3', '-3'], '', 'Sum of oxidation numbers = overall charge.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['The oxidation number of P in P₂O₅ is ___.', ['+5', '5'], '', 'Sum of oxidation numbers = overall charge.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['The oxidation number of Fe in Fe₂O₃ is ___.', ['+3', '3'], '', 'Sum of oxidation numbers = overall charge.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['Balance the redox equation MnO₄⁻ + Fe²⁺ + H⁺ → Mn²⁺ + Fe³⁺ + H₂O using half-equations. Identify the oxidising and reducing agents.', ['Reduction half-equation', 'Oxidation half-equation ×5', 'Overall balanced', 'Oxidising agent', 'Reducing agent'], 'Reduction half-equation: MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O. Oxidation half-equation: Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻ (multiply by 5). Overall: MnO₄⁻ + 5Fe²⁺ + 8H⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 5Fe³⁺ + 4H₂O. MnO₄⁻ is the oxidising agent (Mn goes from +7 to +2); Fe²⁺ is the reducing agent (Fe goes from +2 to +3).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 8],
        ['Define oxidation and reduction in terms of (a) oxygen, (b) hydrogen, (c) electrons, (d) oxidation number. Give one example for any two of these.', ['Oxygen definitions', 'Hydrogen definitions', 'Electron definitions', 'Oxidation number definitions', 'Examples'], '(a) Oxidation is gain of oxygen; reduction is loss of oxygen: 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO (Mg oxidised). (b) Oxidation is loss of hydrogen; reduction is gain of hydrogen: H₂S + Cl₂ → 2HCl + S (H₂S oxidised). (c) Oxidation is loss of electrons; reduction is gain of electrons: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻. (d) Oxidation is an increase in oxidation number; reduction is a decrease: Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['In the reaction 2KI + Cl₂ → 2KCl + I₂: (a) state the oxidation numbers of iodine and chlorine before and after, (b) identify the species oxidised and reduced, (c) write the two half-equations, (d) state the observation.', ['Oxidation number changes', 'Oxidised and reduced', 'Half-equations', 'Brown colour'], '(a) Iodine: −1 in KI to 0 in I₂. Chlorine: 0 in Cl₂ to −1 in KCl. (b) I⁻ is oxidised; Cl₂ is reduced. (c) 2I⁻ → I₂ + 2e⁻ and Cl₂ + 2e⁻ → 2Cl⁻. (d) The colourless solution turns brown as iodine forms.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['Balance the equation Cr₂O₇²⁻ + H⁺ + Fe²⁺ → Cr³⁺ + Fe³⁺ + H₂O by the half-equation method and calculate the moles of Fe²⁺ that react with 0.02 mol of dichromate.', ['Reduction half-equation', 'Oxidation ×6', 'Overall equation', 'Ratio 1 : 6', '0.12 mol'], 'Reduction: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O. Oxidation: Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻ (×6). Overall: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6Fe²⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + 6Fe³⁺ + 7H₂O. The mole ratio of Cr₂O₇²⁻ to Fe²⁺ is 1 : 6, so 0.02 mol of dichromate reacts with 0.12 mol of Fe²⁺.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain, with an example, what is meant by a disproportionation reaction and show the changes in oxidation number.', ['Definition', 'Example equation', 'Oxidation number 0', 'To −1 and +1'], 'In a disproportionation reaction the same element in one species is simultaneously oxidised and reduced. Example: Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaOCl + H₂O. Chlorine starts at 0; in NaCl it is −1 (reduced) and in NaOCl it is +1 (oxidised). Another example: 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂, where oxygen goes from −1 to −2 and to 0.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 5],
    ],
    'SHS 3: Electrochemical cells and electrolysis' => [
        ['Electrolysis is', ['the flow of electrons in a metal', 'the dissolving of a salt', 'the decomposition of an electrolyte by an electric current', 'burning a substance'], 'C', 'Requires ions free to move.', 1],
        ['An electrolyte is a substance that', ['conducts in the solid state', 'never conducts', 'is always a metal', 'conducts electricity when molten or in solution and is decomposed'], 'D', 'For example NaCl(aq).', 1],
        ['During electrolysis, oxidation takes place at the', ['anode', 'cathode', 'both electrodes', 'battery'], 'A', 'An Ox, Red Cat.', 2],
        ['During electrolysis, reduction takes place at the', ['anode', 'cathode', 'salt bridge', 'wire'], 'B', 'Cations gain electrons.', 2],
        ['In the electrolysis of molten lead(II) bromide, the product at the cathode is', ['bromine', 'hydrogen', 'lead', 'oxygen'], 'C', 'Pb²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Pb.', 2],
        ['In the electrolysis of dilute sulphuric acid with inert electrodes, the products are', ['sulphur and oxygen', 'hydrogen at the anode', 'sulphur dioxide', 'hydrogen at the cathode and oxygen at the anode'], 'D', 'Volume ratio 2 : 1.', 3],
        ['In the electrolysis of concentrated sodium chloride solution, the gas at the anode is', ['chlorine', 'oxygen', 'hydrogen', 'sodium'], 'A', 'Concentration effect.', 3],
        ['In the electrolysis of copper(II) sulphate solution with copper electrodes, the anode', ['gains mass', 'dissolves and loses mass', 'gives off oxygen', 'is unchanged'], 'B', 'Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻.', 3],
        ['Electroplating an iron spoon with silver requires the spoon to be the', ['anode', 'electrolyte', 'cathode', 'salt bridge'], 'C', 'Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag on the spoon.', 2],
        ['In a Daniell cell (Zn/Cu), the negative electrode is', ['copper', 'the salt bridge', 'the copper sulphate', 'zinc'], 'D', 'Zinc is more reactive and loses electrons.', 3],
        ['A salt bridge in an electrochemical cell', ['completes the circuit by allowing ions to move between half-cells', 'carries electrons', 'stops the reaction', 'is made of copper'], 'A', 'Often KNO₃-soaked paper.', 3],
        ['The standard hydrogen electrode has an electrode potential of', ['1.00 V', '0.00 V', '−0.76 V', '+0.34 V'], 'B', 'Reference electrode.', 3],
        ['The e.m.f. of a cell is calculated as', ['E(anode) − E(cathode)', 'E(cathode) + E(anode)', 'E(cathode) − E(anode)', 'E(anode) × E(cathode)'], 'C', 'E°cell = E°right − E°left.', 4],
        ['A metal with a more negative standard electrode potential is', ['a stronger oxidising agent', 'less reactive', 'never oxidised', 'a stronger reducing agent'], 'D', 'For example Zn (−0.76 V).', 4],
        ['Copper is purified by electrolysis using', ['impure copper as anode and pure copper as cathode', 'pure copper as anode', 'carbon electrodes only', 'no electrolyte'], 'A', 'Copper(II) sulphate electrolyte.', 3],
        ['Aluminium is extracted by the electrolysis of', ['aluminium chloride solution', 'alumina dissolved in molten cryolite', 'bauxite in water', 'aluminium sulphate solution'], 'B', 'Hall–Héroult process.', 3],
        ['The dry Leclanché cell uses', ['two copper electrodes', 'lead and lead oxide', 'zinc as the negative and carbon as the positive electrode', 'lithium and cobalt'], 'C', 'Ammonium chloride paste electrolyte.', 4],
        ['The lead–acid accumulator is a', ['primary cell', 'fuel cell', 'solar cell', 'secondary (rechargeable) cell'], 'D', 'Used in car batteries.', 3],
        ['In the electrolysis of dilute NaCl solution, the gas at the cathode is', ['hydrogen', 'sodium', 'chlorine', 'oxygen'], 'A', 'H⁺/H₂O discharged before Na⁺.', 3],
        ['Ions are preferentially discharged at the cathode if they are', ['higher in the electrochemical series', 'lower in the electrochemical series', 'anions', 'larger'], 'B', 'For example Cu²⁺ before H⁺.', 4],
        ['Solid sodium chloride conducts electricity.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'Its ions are not free to move.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['In electrolysis, anions move to the anode.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Negative ions are attracted to the positive electrode.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['In a galvanic cell, chemical energy is converted into electrical energy.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Spontaneous redox reaction.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Electrolysis of brine produces chlorine, hydrogen and sodium hydroxide.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Chlor-alkali process.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['The cathode is the positive electrode in electrolysis.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'The cathode is negative in electrolysis.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Fuel cells convert the energy of a fuel directly into electricity.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'For example hydrogen–oxygen cells.', 3, 'tf'],
        ['The positive electrode in electrolysis is the ___.', ['anode'], '', 'Oxidation happens there.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['Coating a metal object with a thin layer of another metal by electrolysis is called ___.', ['electroplating', 'electro-plating'], '', 'The object is the cathode.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Positive ions in electrolysis are called ___.', ['cations'], '', 'They move to the cathode.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V and E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = −0.76 V. The e.m.f. of the Zn/Cu cell is ___ V.', ['1.10', '1.1', '+1.10', '+1.1'], '', '0.34 − (−0.76).', 4, 'fill'],
        ['Aluminium oxide is dissolved in molten ___ to lower its melting point during electrolysis.', ['cryolite', 'Na₃AlF₆', 'Na3AlF6'], '', 'Saves energy.', 4, 'fill'],
        ['The reference electrode with E° = 0.00 V is the standard ___ electrode.', ['hydrogen'], '', 'Platinum in 1 mol/dm³ H⁺ with H₂ at 1 atm.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['Describe the electrolysis of dilute sulphuric acid using platinum electrodes. Write the half-equations at each electrode and explain why the volume of gas at the cathode is twice that at the anode.', ['Ions present', 'Cathode equation', 'Anode equation', '2 : 1 ratio explained', 'Water electrolysed'], 'The ions present are H⁺, SO₄²⁻ and OH⁻ (from water). At the cathode, H⁺ ions are discharged: 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂. At the anode, OH⁻ ions are discharged in preference to SO₄²⁻: 4OH⁻ → 2H₂O + O₂ + 4e⁻. For every 4 electrons, 2 mol of H₂ and 1 mol of O₂ form, so the volume of hydrogen is twice that of oxygen. The acid becomes more concentrated as water is effectively electrolysed.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Draw and label a Daniell cell. Write the half-equations at each electrode and the overall equation. Calculate the cell e.m.f. given E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = −0.76 V and E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V.', ['Labelled diagram with salt bridge', 'Zinc half-equation', 'Copper half-equation', 'Overall equation', '1.10 V'], 'Diagram: a zinc rod in ZnSO₄ solution and a copper rod in CuSO₄ solution, joined by a wire through a voltmeter and connected by a salt bridge. At the zinc (negative) electrode: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ (oxidation). At the copper (positive) electrode: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu (reduction). Overall: Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu. E°cell = +0.34 − (−0.76) = +1.10 V.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Describe how copper is purified by electrolysis. Include the electrodes, the electrolyte, the half-equations and what happens to the impurities.', ['Impure anode, pure cathode', 'CuSO₄ electrolyte', 'Anode equation', 'Cathode equation', 'Anode sludge'], 'The anode is a block of impure copper and the cathode is a thin sheet of pure copper; the electrolyte is acidified copper(II) sulphate solution. At the anode copper dissolves: Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻. At the cathode pure copper is deposited: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu. The anode gets smaller and the cathode grows. Impurities such as silver and gold fall below the anode as anode sludge; more reactive metals dissolve and stay in solution.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain the factors that determine which ions are discharged at the electrodes during electrolysis of aqueous solutions. Use the electrolysis of concentrated and dilute sodium chloride as examples.', ['Electrochemical series', 'Concentration', 'Nature of electrode', 'Cathode H₂ both', 'Anode Cl₂ vs O₂'], 'Three factors: the position of the ion in the electrochemical series (ions lower in the series are discharged more easily), the concentration of the ion (a much more concentrated ion may be discharged in preference), and the nature of the electrode (active electrodes may dissolve). At the cathode in both solutions, H⁺ (from water) is discharged rather than Na⁺, giving hydrogen. At the anode, in concentrated NaCl, Cl⁻ is discharged because of its high concentration, giving chlorine; in very dilute NaCl, OH⁻ is discharged, giving oxygen.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 8],
        ['State three differences between an electrolytic cell and an electrochemical (galvanic) cell.', ['Energy conversion', 'Spontaneous vs non-spontaneous', 'Electrode signs', 'Salt bridge'], 'In an electrolytic cell, electrical energy is converted to chemical energy; in a galvanic cell, chemical energy is converted to electrical energy. Electrolysis drives a non-spontaneous reaction using an external supply; a galvanic cell uses a spontaneous redox reaction. In electrolysis the anode is positive and the cathode negative; in a galvanic cell the anode is negative and the cathode positive. Galvanic cells usually have two half-cells joined by a salt bridge.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
    ],
    'SHS 3: Faraday\'s laws of electrolysis' => [
        ['Faraday\'s first law states that the mass of a substance liberated at an electrode is', ['inversely proportional to the current', 'independent of time', 'proportional to the quantity of electricity passed', 'proportional to the voltage only'], 'C', 'm ∝ Q.', 3],
        ['Faraday\'s second law states that the masses of different substances liberated by the same quantity of electricity are', ['all equal', 'inversely proportional to molar mass', 'unrelated', 'proportional to their molar masses divided by the charge on their ions'], 'D', 'Equivalent masses.', 4],
        ['One faraday is the charge on', ['one mole of electrons, about 96,500 C', 'one electron', 'one coulomb', 'one ampere'], 'A', 'F = Le.', 2],
        ['The quantity of electricity Q (in coulombs) equals', ['current ÷ time', 'current × time in seconds', 'voltage × current', 'time ÷ current'], 'B', 'Q = It.', 1],
        ['To deposit 1 mol of copper from Cu²⁺ ions requires', ['1 faraday', '3 faradays', '2 faradays', '0.5 faraday'], 'C', 'Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu.', 3],
        ['To deposit 1 mol of aluminium from Al³⁺ ions requires', ['1 faraday', '2 faradays', '6 faradays', '3 faradays'], 'D', 'Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al.', 3],
        ['The same charge that deposits 1 mol of silver will deposit', ['0.5 mol of copper', '1 mol of copper', '2 mol of copper', '3 mol of copper'], 'A', 'Ag⁺ needs 1e⁻; Cu²⁺ needs 2e⁻.', 4],
        ['Doubling the current while keeping the time constant', ['halves the mass deposited', 'doubles the mass deposited', 'has no effect', 'quadruples the mass deposited'], 'B', 'm ∝ It.', 2],
        ['A current of 5 A flows for 10 minutes. How much charge passes?', ['50 C', '6,000 C', '3,000 C', '1,500 C'], 'C', 'Q = It = 5 × 10 × 60.', 2],
        ['A current of 5 A is passed through a solution containing Zn²⁺ ions for 32 minutes. What mass of zinc is deposited? (zinc = 65, F = 96,500 C/mol)', ['6.47 g', '1.62 g', '6.57 g', '3.23 g'], 'D', 'n(e⁻) = 9,600 ÷ 96,500; n(zinc) = n(e⁻) ÷ 2; × 65.', 5],
        ['A current of 2.5 A is passed through a solution containing Zn²⁺ ions for 30 minutes. What mass of zinc is deposited? (zinc = 65, F = 96,500 C/mol)', ['1.52 g', '3.03 g', '0.76 g', '3.13 g'], 'A', 'n(e⁻) = 4,500 ÷ 96,500; n(zinc) = n(e⁻) ÷ 2; × 65.', 5],
        ['A current of 2 A is passed through a solution containing Ag⁺ ions for 16 minutes. What mass of silver is deposited? (silver = 108, F = 96,500 C/mol)', ['6.45 g', '2.15 g', '4.4 g', '3.15 g'], 'B', 'n(e⁻) = 1,920 ÷ 96,500; n(silver) = n(e⁻) ÷ 1; × 108.', 5],
        ['How many coulombs are needed to deposit 0.05 mol of zinc from Zn²⁺ ions? (F = 96,500 C/mol)', ['4,825 C', '28,950 C', '9,650 C', '106,150 C'], 'C', 'Q = n × 2 × 96,500.', 4],
        ['A current of 0.5 A flows for 64 minutes. How much charge passes?', ['32 C', '3,840 C', '960 C', '1,920 C'], 'D', 'Q = It = 0.5 × 64 × 60.', 2],
        ['A current of 5 A flows for 30 minutes. How much charge passes?', ['9,000 C', '150 C', '18,000 C', '4,500 C'], 'A', 'Q = It = 5 × 30 × 60.', 2],
        ['A current of 1 A flows for 30 minutes. How much charge passes?', ['30 C', '1,800 C', '3,600 C', '900 C'], 'B', 'Q = It = 1 × 30 × 60.', 2],
        ['A current of 0.5 A is passed through a solution containing Cu²⁺ ions for 10 minutes. What mass of copper is deposited? (copper = 64, F = 96,500 C/mol)', ['0.2 g', '0.05 g', '0.1 g', '0.3 g'], 'C', 'n(e⁻) = 300 ÷ 96,500; n(copper) = n(e⁻) ÷ 2; × 64.', 5],
        ['A current of 5 A is passed through a solution containing Zn²⁺ ions for 10 minutes. What mass of zinc is deposited? (zinc = 65, F = 96,500 C/mol)', ['2.02 g', '0.51 g', '2.12 g', '1.01 g'], 'D', 'n(e⁻) = 3,000 ÷ 96,500; n(zinc) = n(e⁻) ÷ 2; × 65.', 5],
        ['A current of 4 A flows for 16 minutes. How much charge passes?', ['3,840 C', '64 C', '7,680 C', '1,920 C'], 'A', 'Q = It = 4 × 16 × 60.', 2],
        ['A current of 2 A is passed through a solution containing Al³⁺ ions for 30 minutes. What mass of aluminium is deposited? (aluminium = 27, F = 96,500 C/mol)', ['1.01 g', '0.34 g', '0.11 g', '0.77 g'], 'B', 'n(e⁻) = 3,600 ÷ 96,500; n(aluminium) = n(e⁻) ÷ 3; × 27.', 5],
        ['The faraday constant is about 96,500 C/mol.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Charge on 1 mol of electrons.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['Q = I ÷ t.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'Q = I × t.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['More charge is needed to deposit 1 mol of Al than 1 mol of Na.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Al³⁺ needs 3 electrons; Na⁺ needs 1.', 3, 'tf'],
        ['The time in Q = It must be in minutes.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'It must be in seconds.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['At s.t.p., 2 faradays liberate 22.4 dm³ of hydrogen.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', '2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂: 1 mol H₂.', 4, 'tf'],
        ['Doubling the time of electrolysis doubles the mass deposited at constant current.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'm ∝ t.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['A current of 5 A flowing for 100 s delivers ___ C of charge.', ['500'], '', 'Q = It.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['A current of 3 A flowing for 965 s delivers ___ C of charge.', ['2,895', '2895'], '', 'Q = It.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['A current of 3 A flowing for 200 s delivers ___ C of charge.', ['600'], '', 'Q = It.', 1, 'fill'],
