        ['Two cells containing copper(II) sulphate and silver nitrate solutions are connected in series. A current of 3 A is passed for 20 minutes. (Cu = 64, Ag = 108, F = 96,500 C/mol) (a) State Faraday\'s second law. (b) Calculate the quantity of electricity passed. (c) Calculate the mass of copper deposited. (d) Calculate the mass of silver deposited.', ['Second law', 'Q = It', 'Mass of copper 1.19 g', 'Mass of silver 4.03 g'], '(a) When the same quantity of electricity passes through different electrolytes, the masses of the elements liberated are proportional to their molar masses divided by the charges on their ions. (b) Q = 3 × 20 × 60 = 3,600 C. (c) Moles of electrons = 3,600 ÷ 96,500 = 0.0373; Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, so moles Cu = 0.0187; mass = 0.0187 × 64 = 1.19 g. (d) Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag, so moles Ag = 0.0373; mass = 0.0373 × 108 = 4.03 g.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 8],
        ['Two cells containing copper(II) sulphate and silver nitrate solutions are connected in series. A current of 2 A is passed for 20 minutes. (Cu = 64, Ag = 108, F = 96,500 C/mol) (a) State Faraday\'s second law. (b) Calculate the quantity of electricity passed. (c) Calculate the mass of copper deposited. (d) Calculate the mass of silver deposited.', ['Second law', 'Q = It', 'Mass of copper 0.80 g', 'Mass of silver 2.69 g'], '(a) When the same quantity of electricity passes through different electrolytes, the masses of the elements liberated are proportional to their molar masses divided by the charges on their ions. (b) Q = 2 × 20 × 60 = 2,400 C. (c) Moles of electrons = 2,400 ÷ 96,500 = 0.0249; Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, so moles Cu = 0.0124; mass = 0.0124 × 64 = 0.80 g. (d) Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag, so moles Ag = 0.0249; mass = 0.0249 × 108 = 2.69 g.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 8],
        ['Two cells containing copper(II) sulphate and silver nitrate solutions are connected in series. A current of 5 A is passed for 30 minutes. (Cu = 64, Ag = 108, F = 96,500 C/mol) (a) State Faraday\'s second law. (b) Calculate the quantity of electricity passed. (c) Calculate the mass of copper deposited. (d) Calculate the mass of silver deposited.', ['Second law', 'Q = It', 'Mass of copper 2.98 g', 'Mass of silver 10.07 g'], '(a) When the same quantity of electricity passes through different electrolytes, the masses of the elements liberated are proportional to their molar masses divided by the charges on their ions. (b) Q = 5 × 30 × 60 = 9,000 C. (c) Moles of electrons = 9,000 ÷ 96,500 = 0.0933; Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, so moles Cu = 0.0466; mass = 0.0466 × 64 = 2.98 g. (d) Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag, so moles Ag = 0.0933; mass = 0.0933 × 108 = 10.07 g.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 8],
        ['Two cells containing copper(II) sulphate and silver nitrate solutions are connected in series. A current of 2 A is passed for 30 minutes. (Cu = 64, Ag = 108, F = 96,500 C/mol) (a) State Faraday\'s second law. (b) Calculate the quantity of electricity passed. (c) Calculate the mass of copper deposited. (d) Calculate the mass of silver deposited.', ['Second law', 'Q = It', 'Mass of copper 1.19 g', 'Mass of silver 4.03 g'], '(a) When the same quantity of electricity passes through different electrolytes, the masses of the elements liberated are proportional to their molar masses divided by the charges on their ions. (b) Q = 2 × 30 × 60 = 3,600 C. (c) Moles of electrons = 3,600 ÷ 96,500 = 0.0373; Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, so moles Cu = 0.0187; mass = 0.0187 × 64 = 1.19 g. (d) Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag, so moles Ag = 0.0373; mass = 0.0373 × 108 = 4.03 g.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 8],
        ['Two cells containing copper(II) sulphate and silver nitrate solutions are connected in series. A current of 5 A is passed for 40 minutes. (Cu = 64, Ag = 108, F = 96,500 C/mol) (a) State Faraday\'s second law. (b) Calculate the quantity of electricity passed. (c) Calculate the mass of copper deposited. (d) Calculate the mass of silver deposited.', ['Second law', 'Q = It', 'Mass of copper 3.98 g', 'Mass of silver 13.43 g'], '(a) When the same quantity of electricity passes through different electrolytes, the masses of the elements liberated are proportional to their molar masses divided by the charges on their ions. (b) Q = 5 × 40 × 60 = 12,000 C. (c) Moles of electrons = 12,000 ÷ 96,500 = 0.1244; Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, so moles Cu = 0.0622; mass = 0.0622 × 64 = 3.98 g. (d) Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag, so moles Ag = 0.1244; mass = 0.1244 × 108 = 13.43 g.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 8],
    ],
    'SHS 3: Transition metals and complex ions' => [
        ['Transition elements are d-block elements that', ['have a full d subshell in all their ions', 'are all non-metals', 'form at least one stable ion with a partly filled d subshell', 'have no d electrons'], 'C', 'Zinc and scandium are usually excluded.', 3],
        ['The electron configuration of iron (Z = 26) is', ['[Ar] 3d⁸', '[Ar] 4s² 4p⁶', '[Ar] 3d⁵ 4s³', '[Ar] 3d⁶ 4s²'], 'D', '4s fills before 3d.', 4],
        ['The electron configuration of chromium (Z = 24) is', ['[Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹', '[Ar] 3d⁴ 4s²', '[Ar] 3d⁶', '[Ar] 4s² 4p⁴'], 'A', 'A half-filled d subshell is extra stable.', 5],
        ['The electron configuration of copper (Z = 29) is', ['[Ar] 3d⁹ 4s²', '[Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹', '[Ar] 3d¹¹', '[Ar] 4s² 4p⁹'], 'B', 'A full d subshell is extra stable.', 5],
        ['When transition metals form ions, electrons are lost first from the', ['3d subshell', '3p subshell', '4s subshell', 'nucleus'], 'C', 'Fe²⁺ is [Ar] 3d⁶.', 4],
        ['Transition metal compounds are often coloured because', ['they contain water', 'they are metals', 'their s electrons are excited', 'electrons move between split d orbitals, absorbing visible light'], 'D', 'The complementary colour is seen.', 4],
        ['Transition metals are useful catalysts because they', ['can show variable oxidation states and adsorb reactants', 'are always liquid', 'have no electrons', 'are unreactive'], 'A', 'For example Fe in the Haber process.', 3],
        ['A complex ion consists of', ['two metals bonded together', 'a central metal ion surrounded by ligands', 'an anion only', 'a molecule with no metal'], 'B', 'For example [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺.', 3],
        ['A ligand is', ['a metal atom', 'a positive ion only', 'a molecule or ion with a lone pair that forms a dative bond to a metal ion', 'an electron'], 'C', 'For example H₂O, NH₃, Cl⁻, CN⁻.', 3],
        ['The coordination number of the central ion in [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ is', ['2', '4', '1', '6'], 'D', 'Six ligands.', 3],
        ['The shape of a complex ion with coordination number 6 is usually', ['octahedral', 'tetrahedral', 'linear', 'square planar'], 'A', '90° angles.', 4],
        ['The oxidation state of iron in [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ is', ['+2', '+3', '−3', '+6'], 'B', 'x + 6(−1) = −3.', 4],
        ['The deep blue complex formed when excess ammonia is added to Cu²⁺(aq) is', ['[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺', 'Cu(OH)₂', '[Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺', '[CuCl₄]²⁻'], 'C', 'Ligand substitution.', 5],
        ['Iron shows the common oxidation states', ['+1 only', '+4 only', '−2 and −3', '+2 and +3'], 'D', 'Fe²⁺ green, Fe³⁺ yellow-brown.', 2],
        ['The colour of aqueous Fe²⁺ ions is', ['pale green', 'yellow-brown', 'blue', 'purple'], 'A', '[Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺.', 2],
        ['The colour of aqueous Cu²⁺ ions is', ['green', 'blue', 'colourless', 'yellow'], 'B', '[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺.', 1],
        ['Zinc is not regarded as a transition metal because', ['it is a non-metal', 'it has no d electrons', 'its only ion, Zn²⁺, has a full 3d subshell', 'it is in group 2'], 'C', '[Ar] 3d¹⁰.', 4],
        ['A bidentate ligand', ['forms one bond', 'has no lone pairs', 'is always a metal', 'forms two dative bonds to the same metal ion'], 'D', 'For example ethanedioate or ethane-1,2-diamine.', 5],
        ['The highest oxidation state of manganese is', ['+7', '+2', '+4', '+3'], 'A', 'In MnO₄⁻.', 3],
        ['Compared with group 1 metals, transition metals', ['are softer', 'have higher melting points and densities', 'are more reactive with water', 'form only +1 ions'], 'B', 'Strong metallic bonding.', 2],
        ['Transition metals can show more than one oxidation state.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'For example Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['Scandium is coloured in its compounds because Sc³⁺ has d electrons.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'Sc³⁺ has no d electrons; its compounds are colourless.', 5, 'tf'],
        ['Water can act as a ligand.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'It has lone pairs on oxygen.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['The 4s electrons are removed before the 3d electrons when transition metals form ions.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Cu²⁺ is [Ar] 3d⁹.', 4, 'tf'],
        ['Transition metals are poor conductors of electricity.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'They are good conductors.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['Platinum is used as a catalyst in catalytic converters.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'With rhodium and palladium.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Molecules or ions that donate a lone pair to a central metal ion are called ___.', ['ligands'], '', 'For example NH₃.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['The number of dative bonds to the central ion in a complex is its ___ number.', ['coordination', 'co-ordination'], '', 'For example 6 in [Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['The oxidation state of copper in [CuCl₄]²⁻ is ___.', ['+2', '2'], '', 'x + 4(−1) = −2.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['The electron configuration of Fe³⁺ is [Ar] 3d___.', ['5', '⁵'], '', 'Fe [Ar] 3d⁶ 4s² loses 4s² and one 3d.', 4, 'fill'],
        ['The catalyst used in the Contact process, V₂O₅, contains vanadium in the +___ oxidation state.', ['5'], '', '2x + 5(−2) = 0.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['A complex with four ligands arranged at the corners of a square has a square ___ shape.', ['planar'], '', 'For example [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂].', 4, 'fill'],
        ['State four characteristic properties of transition metals and explain any two in terms of electron structure.', ['Four properties', 'Variable oxidation states explained', 'Colour explained', 'Catalysis or complex formation'], 'Properties: variable oxidation states; formation of coloured compounds; formation of complex ions; catalytic activity; also high melting points and densities. Variable oxidation states: the 4s and 3d electrons have similar energies, so different numbers can be lost (Fe²⁺, Fe³⁺). Colour: in complexes the d orbitals split into two energy levels; d electrons absorb visible light of a particular frequency to move to the higher level, and the complementary colour is seen. Catalysis: they can change oxidation state easily and provide surfaces for adsorption.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Write the electron configurations of Fe, Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺. Explain why Fe³⁺ is more stable than Fe²⁺.', ['Fe configuration', 'Fe²⁺', 'Fe³⁺', 'Half-filled d⁵ stable'], 'Fe: [Ar] 3d⁶ 4s². Fe²⁺: [Ar] 3d⁶ (the two 4s electrons are lost first). Fe³⁺: [Ar] 3d⁵. Fe³⁺ has a half-filled d subshell, with one electron in each of the five d orbitals; this symmetrical arrangement is especially stable, so Fe²⁺ is easily oxidised to Fe³⁺ in air.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['For the complex ion [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺: (a) name the central ion and its oxidation state, (b) name the ligands, (c) state the coordination number and shape, (d) describe how it is formed from aqueous copper(II) ions.', ['Cu²⁺ +2', 'Ligands', '6 and octahedral', 'Pale blue precipitate', 'Deep blue in excess NH₃'], '(a) Copper(II), oxidation state +2. (b) Ammonia (four) and water (two). (c) Coordination number 6; octahedral (distorted). (d) Adding a little aqueous ammonia to blue [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ gives a pale blue precipitate of copper(II) hydroxide; with excess ammonia, the precipitate dissolves and four water ligands are replaced by ammonia, giving a deep blue solution.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain, with examples, why transition metals and their compounds are good catalysts. Give three industrial examples.', ['Variable oxidation states', 'Surface adsorption', 'Lower activation energy', 'Three examples'], 'Transition metals have variable oxidation states, so they can accept and donate electrons, providing an alternative reaction pathway with lower activation energy. Their surfaces can adsorb reactant molecules, weakening bonds and bringing molecules together. Examples: iron in the Haber process for ammonia; vanadium(V) oxide in the Contact process for sulphuric acid; nickel in the hydrogenation of vegetable oils; platinum/rhodium in catalytic converters and the Ostwald process.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain why chromium and copper have unusual electron configurations. Write their configurations.', ['Cr configuration', 'Cu configuration', 'Close energy levels', 'Half-filled/full stability'], 'Chromium (Z = 24) is [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹ rather than [Ar] 3d⁴ 4s², and copper (Z = 29) is [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹ rather than [Ar] 3d⁹ 4s². The 3d and 4s energies are very close, and a half-filled (d⁵) or completely filled (d¹⁰) d subshell is especially stable, so one 4s electron moves into the 3d subshell.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 5],
    ],
