        ['The ester formed from ethanol and ethanoic acid is ethyl ___.', ['ethanoate', 'acetate'], '', 'CH₃COOC₂H₅.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Describe how soap is made in the laboratory from palm oil. Write a word equation and explain the purpose of adding common salt.', ['Boil oil with NaOH', 'Word equation', 'Glycerol by-product', 'Salting out explained', 'Collect soap'], 'Palm oil is boiled with concentrated sodium hydroxide solution, stirring for some time; the fat is hydrolysed. Word equation: fat (oil) + sodium hydroxide → soap (sodium salt of fatty acid) + glycerol. Common salt (brine) is added to the hot mixture; soap is less soluble in salt solution, so it separates and floats as a solid layer (salting out). The soap is skimmed off, washed and moulded.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain, with a labelled diagram of a soap molecule, how soap removes grease from cloth.', ['Labelled molecule', 'Hydrophobic tail in grease', 'Hydrophilic head in water', 'Micelles', 'Rinsed away'], 'A soap molecule has a long hydrocarbon tail, which is hydrophobic, and an ionic carboxylate head, –COO⁻Na⁺, which is hydrophilic. In washing, the tails dissolve in the grease while the heads stay in the water. Agitation breaks the grease into small droplets surrounded by soap molecules (micelles) with negative heads outward. The droplets repel each other and are carried away in the water, so the cloth becomes clean.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Compare soaps and soapless detergents under the headings: raw materials, action in hard water, biodegradability and effect on the environment.', ['Raw materials', 'Hard water', 'Biodegradability', 'Environmental effects'], 'Raw materials: soaps are made from fats and oils (renewable); soapless detergents from petroleum products. Hard water: soap forms scum with Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ and is wasted; detergents do not form scum because their calcium salts are soluble. Biodegradability: soaps are biodegradable; some detergents with branched chains are not, causing foam in rivers. Environment: phosphates added to detergents cause eutrophication; soap causes fewer problems.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Write the structural formula of a triglyceride in general form and write an equation for its saponification with sodium hydroxide.', ['Triglyceride structure', '3NaOH', '3RCOONa soap', 'Glycerol'], 'A triglyceride has a glycerol backbone in which each –OH has been esterified: CH₂OCOR / CHOCOR / CH₂OCOR, where R is a long hydrocarbon chain. Saponification: (RCOO)₃C₃H₅ + 3NaOH → 3RCOONa + C₃H₅(OH)₃. The products are three molecules of soap (sodium salt of the fatty acid) and one molecule of glycerol.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['Describe the local production of alata samina (black soap) in Ghana and compare it with industrial soap making.', ['Burn plant waste to ash', 'Extract potash lye', 'Boil with palm oil', 'Potassium soap is soft', 'Industrial comparison'], 'Dried plantain peels, cocoa pod husks or palm leaves are burnt to ash. Water is passed through the ash to dissolve the potassium compounds, giving a potash solution (lye). The lye is boiled with palm oil or palm kernel oil and stirred until it thickens into soft soap, which is left to cure. Industrial soap uses pure sodium hydroxide, controlled temperatures, salting out and additives (perfumes, colours); local soap is softer (a potassium soap) and retains glycerol.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 5],
    ],
    'SHS 3: Addition and condensation polymers' => [
        ['A polymer is', ['a small molecule', 'a mixture of metals', 'an element', 'a large molecule made from many small repeating units called monomers'], 'D', 'Poly = many.', 1],
