        ['PVC is used for water pipes and electrical insulation.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'It is strong and a good insulator.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['The small molecules that join to form a polymer are called ___.', ['monomers'], '', 'For example ethene.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['The monomer of PVC is chloro___.', ['ethene'], '', 'CH₂=CHCl.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Heating rubber with sulphur to strengthen it is called ___.', ['vulcanisation', 'vulcanization'], '', 'Forms S–S cross-links.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['Nylon and proteins both contain the ___ linkage.', ['amide', 'peptide'], '', '–CONH–.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['A plastic that hardens permanently on heating is a ___ plastic.', ['thermosetting', 'thermoset'], '', 'For example Bakelite.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Polymerisation in which a small molecule like water is eliminated is called ___ polymerisation.', ['condensation'], '', 'For example nylon.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Distinguish between addition and condensation polymerisation, giving one example of each with the monomer(s) and a structural equation.', ['Addition definition', 'Addition example and equation', 'Condensation definition', 'Condensation example', 'By-product named'], 'In addition polymerisation, unsaturated monomers with C=C bonds join together without losing any atoms; the polymer has the same empirical formula as the monomer. Example: n CH₂=CH₂ → –(CH₂–CH₂)ₙ– (polyethene). In condensation polymerisation, monomers with two functional groups join with the elimination of a small molecule such as water or HCl. Example: a diamine and a dicarboxylic acid form nylon, with amide links –CONH– and loss of water; or a diol and a dicarboxylic acid form Terylene (polyester).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Compare thermoplastics and thermosetting plastics in terms of structure, behaviour on heating and uses.', ['Thermoplastic structure', 'Softens and remoulds', 'Thermoset cross-links', 'Does not soften', 'Uses of each'], 'Thermoplastics have long chains with weak forces between them and no cross-links, so they soften when heated and can be remoulded many times; they are used for bottles, bags and pipes (polyethene, PVC). Thermosetting plastics have strong covalent cross-links between chains, so once set they do not soften on heating but char; they are used for electrical fittings, pan handles and worktops (Bakelite, melamine).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Discuss the problems caused by plastic waste in Ghana and suggest four ways of reducing them.', ['Non-biodegradable', 'Blocked drains and flooding', 'Toxic gases when burnt', 'Four solutions'], 'Most plastics are non-biodegradable, so sachet water bags and bottles litter streets, block gutters and drains, causing flooding and breeding mosquitoes. Animals may eat them and die; burning releases toxic gases such as dioxins and HCl. Ways to reduce: recycling plastics into new products; reusing bags and containers; using biodegradable plastics; better waste collection and sorting; laws and levies on single-use plastics; public education.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Name four natural polymers, state the monomer of each and give one use.', ['Starch', 'Cellulose', 'Protein', 'Rubber or DNA'], 'Starch: glucose; food energy store in cassava, yam and maize. Cellulose: glucose; plant cell walls, used to make paper and cotton cloth. Proteins: amino acids; body-building, found in meat, beans and silk. Natural rubber: isoprene; tyres, rubber bands (after vulcanisation). DNA: nucleotides; genetic information.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Draw the repeat unit and name the monomer of (a) polyethene, (b) PVC, (c) polypropene, (d) PTFE. State one use of each polymer.', ['Polyethene', 'PVC', 'Polypropene', 'PTFE', 'Uses'], '(a) –CH₂–CH₂–, monomer ethene; plastic bags and bottles. (b) –CH₂–CHCl–, monomer chloroethene; pipes, cable insulation. (c) –CH₂–CH(CH₃)–, monomer propene; ropes, crates, carpets. (d) –CF₂–CF₂–, monomer tetrafluoroethene; non-stick pan coatings.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
