        ['Gold is found native (uncombined) because it is unreactive.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Low in the reactivity series.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['Alloys are usually harder than the pure metals.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Different sized atoms stop layers sliding.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['Painting iron prevents rusting by keeping out air and water.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'A barrier method.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['The Haber process uses a vanadium(V) oxide catalyst.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'It uses iron; V₂O₅ is used in the Contact process.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['The main ore of iron is ___.', ['haematite', 'hematite'], '', 'Fe₂O₃.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['The impurity formed from limestone and sand in the blast furnace is called ___.', ['slag', 'calcium silicate'], '', 'CaSiO₃.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Coating iron with zinc to prevent rusting is called ___.', ['galvanising', 'galvanizing', 'galvanisation'], '', 'Sacrificial protection.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['The process that makes sulphuric acid is the ___ process.', ['Contact'], '', 'Uses V₂O₅.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['An alloy of copper and zinc is ___.', ['brass'], '', 'Gold-coloured.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['The dam at Akosombo provides electricity for aluminium smelting at the ___ smelter in Tema.', ['VALCO', 'Valco'], '', 'Volta Aluminium Company.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['Describe the extraction of iron from haematite in the blast furnace. Write equations for the main reactions and explain the role of limestone.', ['Raw materials', 'Combustion of coke', 'Formation of CO', 'Reduction equation', 'Limestone and slag'], 'Iron ore (haematite, Fe₂O₃), coke and limestone are fed in at the top; hot air is blown in at the bottom. Coke burns: C + O₂ → CO₂ (gives heat). CO₂ is reduced by more coke: CO₂ + C → 2CO. Carbon monoxide reduces the ore: Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂. Molten iron collects at the bottom. Limestone decomposes: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂; calcium oxide reacts with sandy impurities: CaO + SiO₂ → CaSiO₃ (slag), which floats on the iron and is tapped off separately.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 10],
        ['Describe the Contact process for the manufacture of sulphuric acid. Include the equations, the catalyst and the conditions, and explain why SO₃ is not dissolved directly in water.', ['Burning sulphur', 'Catalytic oxidation with conditions', 'Absorption in conc. H₂SO₄', 'Dilution of oleum', 'Reason for not using water'], 'Sulphur is burnt in air: S + O₂ → SO₂. Sulphur dioxide is oxidised: 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, using a vanadium(V) oxide catalyst at about 450 °C and 1–2 atm (ΔH negative; a compromise temperature). SO₃ is absorbed in concentrated sulphuric acid to form oleum: SO₃ + H₂SO₄ → H₂S₂O₇. Oleum is diluted with water: H₂S₂O₇ + H₂O → 2H₂SO₄. SO₃ is not dissolved directly in water because the reaction is very exothermic and forms a fine mist of acid that is difficult to collect.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 10],
        ['Explain why aluminium is extracted by electrolysis and not by reduction with carbon. Describe the electrolysis of alumina, including the electrode reactions.', ['Reactivity reason', 'Cryolite', 'Cathode equation', 'Anode equation', 'Anodes replaced'], 'Aluminium is high in the reactivity series, more reactive than carbon, so carbon cannot remove oxygen from aluminium oxide. Purified alumina is dissolved in molten cryolite (Na₃AlF₆) to lower the melting point and is electrolysed in a cell with carbon electrodes. At the cathode: Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al (molten aluminium is tapped off). At the anode: 2O²⁻ → O₂ + 4e⁻; the oxygen reacts with the carbon anodes, which burn away and must be replaced.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain the conditions necessary for rusting and describe three methods of preventing it, explaining how each works.', ['Oxygen and water needed', 'Barrier method', 'Galvanising', 'Sacrificial protection'], 'Iron rusts when exposed to both oxygen (air) and water; salt speeds it up. Rust is hydrated iron(III) oxide. Prevention: painting, oiling or greasing, or plastic coating keeps out air and water; galvanising coats iron with zinc, which acts as a barrier and corrodes in preference if scratched; sacrificial protection attaches a more reactive metal such as magnesium, which is oxidised instead of the iron; alloying to make stainless steel.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Discuss the environmental effects of illegal small-scale gold mining (galamsey) in Ghana from a chemical point of view.', ['Mercury poisoning', 'Cyanide', 'Acid drainage and heavy metals', 'Turbid rivers', 'Land degradation and solutions'], 'Mercury used to form an amalgam with gold is released into rivers and soil; it is toxic and accumulates in fish and people, damaging the nervous system. Cyanide spills poison water. Digging exposes sulphide minerals that oxidise to produce acid mine drainage, releasing heavy metals. Rivers such as the Pra and Ankobra become muddy, raising turbidity and making water treatment costly. Land is degraded and forests destroyed. Solutions include regulation, mercury-free methods and land reclamation.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
    ],
