        ['Explain how acid rain is formed. Give equations and describe three effects of acid rain and two ways of reducing it.', ['SO₂ source and equation', 'Sulphuric acid formation', 'Nitric acid formation', 'Three effects', 'Two remedies'], 'Burning fossil fuels containing sulphur releases SO₂: S + O₂ → SO₂. It dissolves in rain water and is oxidised: 2SO₂ + O₂ + 2H₂O → 2H₂SO₄. Nitrogen oxides from vehicle engines form nitric acid: 4NO₂ + O₂ + 2H₂O → 4HNO₃. Effects: damages buildings and statues made of limestone and marble; acidifies lakes, killing fish; leaches nutrients from soil and damages trees; corrodes metal structures. Reduction: removing sulphur from fuels; flue-gas desulphurisation with lime; catalytic converters; using renewable energy.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain the greenhouse effect and state three human activities in Ghana that increase it. Suggest three measures to reduce global warming.', ['Explanation of trapping', 'Greenhouse gases named', 'Three activities', 'Three measures'], 'The Sun\'s short-wave radiation passes through the atmosphere and warms the Earth\'s surface. The Earth emits long-wave infrared radiation, which greenhouse gases such as CO₂, CH₄ and water vapour absorb and re-emit, trapping heat and warming the atmosphere. Activities: burning fossil fuels in vehicles and power plants; deforestation and bush burning; charcoal production; decomposing waste in landfills producing methane. Measures: afforestation; using solar and hydro power; improving public transport; reducing bush burning; efficient cookstoves.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Describe the causes and effects of water pollution in Ghana and suggest how it can be controlled.', ['Causes', 'Health effects', 'Ecological effects', 'Control measures'], 'Causes: galamsey releasing mercury and sediment; dumping of sewage and refuse into rivers and lagoons such as the Korle Lagoon; fertiliser and pesticide run-off from farms; industrial effluents; oil spills. Effects: water-borne diseases such as cholera and typhoid; death of fish and aquatic life; eutrophication; higher cost of water treatment; heavy metal poisoning. Control: proper sewage treatment; banning illegal mining; regulating industrial discharge; public education; correct use of fertilisers.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain how CFCs destroy the ozone layer, with equations, and state the effects of ozone depletion on human health.', ['UV breaks C–Cl', 'Cl radical + O₃', 'Regeneration and chain', 'Health effects'], 'UV light breaks C–Cl bonds in CFCs in the stratosphere, releasing chlorine free radicals: CF₂Cl₂ → CF₂Cl• + Cl•. The chlorine radical reacts with ozone: Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂, and ClO• + O → Cl• + O₂. The chlorine radical is regenerated, so one radical can destroy thousands of ozone molecules (chain reaction). More UV reaches the Earth, increasing skin cancer, cataracts, sunburn and weakening of the immune system.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['Distinguish between biodegradable and non-biodegradable wastes, giving two examples of each, and describe three proper ways of disposing of solid waste.', ['Definitions', 'Two examples each', 'Composting', 'Recycling', 'Landfill or incineration'], 'Biodegradable wastes can be broken down by microorganisms into simple substances, for example food leftovers, paper and leaves. Non-biodegradable wastes cannot be broken down easily, for example plastics, glass and metal cans. Proper disposal: composting biodegradable waste for manure; recycling plastics, glass and metals; sanitary landfill sites; incineration under controlled conditions; reusing containers.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 2, 'theory', 6],
    ],
