        ['Complete and explain the nuclear equations: (a) ²³⁸₉₂U → ²³⁴₉₀Th + ?, (b) ²³⁴₉₀Th → ²³⁴₉₁Pa + ?, (c) ¹⁴₆C → ? + ⁰₋₁e.', ['Alpha identified', 'Beta identified', 'Nitrogen-14', 'Balancing rule'], '(a) The missing particle has mass number 238 − 234 = 4 and atomic number 92 − 90 = 2: ⁴₂He, an alpha particle. (b) Mass number unchanged and atomic number increased by 1, so the missing particle is ⁰₋₁e, a beta particle. (c) Mass number stays 14 and atomic number becomes 6 + 1 = 7: ¹⁴₇N, nitrogen-14. In each equation mass numbers and atomic numbers balance.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['A sample of a radioactive isotope has an activity of 800 counts per minute. After 24 hours its activity is 100 counts per minute. (a) Define half-life. (b) Calculate the half-life. (c) Find the activity after a further 16 hours.', ['Definition', 'Three half-lives', 'Half-life 8 h', '25 counts per minute'], '(a) Half-life is the time taken for half of the radioactive atoms in a sample to decay (or for the activity to fall to half). (b) 800 → 400 → 200 → 100 is 3 half-lives in 24 hours, so the half-life is 8 hours. (c) 16 hours is 2 more half-lives: 100 → 50 → 25 counts per minute.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['State four peaceful uses of radioisotopes and two dangers of radiation. Describe two safety precautions when handling radioactive materials.', ['Four uses', 'Two dangers', 'Tongs or distance', 'Lead shielding or badges'], 'Uses: cancer treatment (cobalt-60 radiotherapy); medical tracers (iodine-131 for thyroid); carbon-14 dating of archaeological samples; sterilising medical instruments and preserving food; detecting leaks in pipes; measuring the thickness of paper or metal sheets; generating electricity in nuclear power stations. Dangers: damage to cells and DNA causing cancer and genetic mutations; radiation burns and radiation sickness. Precautions: handle with long tongs; store in lead-lined containers; wear protective clothing and film badges; limit exposure time and keep a distance.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Distinguish between nuclear fission and nuclear fusion, with an example equation of each, and explain why both release large amounts of energy.', ['Fission definition and equation', 'Fusion definition and equation', 'Mass defect', 'E = mc²'], 'Fission is the splitting of a heavy nucleus into two lighter nuclei when struck by a neutron: ²³⁵₉₂U + ¹₀n → ¹⁴¹₅₆Ba + ⁹²₃₆Kr + 3¹₀n. Fusion is the joining of light nuclei to form a heavier one at very high temperature: ²₁H + ³₁H → ⁴₂He + ¹₀n. In both, the total mass of the products is slightly less than that of the reactants; this mass defect is converted to energy by E = mc², and because c² is very large the energy released is enormous.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
